'''Andrew Albert Christian Edward Mountbatten-Windsor''' (born 19 February 1960), formerly '''Prince Andrew, Duke of York''', is the third child and second son of [[Queen Elizabeth II]] and [[Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh]], and a younger brother of [[King Charles III]]. Andrew was born second in the [[line of succession to the British throne]] and is eighth as of 2026.

Serving in the [[Royal Navy]] from 1979 to 2001, Andrew saw active duty as a helicopter pilot during the [[Falklands War]]; he later became a helicopter instructor and commanded a warship. He married [[Sarah Ferguson]] in 1986, and was created [[Duke of York]] on [[Wedding of Prince Andrew and Sarah Ferguson|their wedding day]]. They had two daughters, [[Princess Beatrice|Beatrice]] and [[Princess Eugenie|Eugenie]], before separating in 1992 and divorcing in 1996. From 2001 to 2011, Andrew served as the UK's [[Special Representative for International Trade and Investment]], but resigned following scrutiny of his expenses and associations with controversial figures. He continued to undertake official duties on behalf of Elizabeth II until 2019.

Andrew had [[Relationship of Prince Andrew and Jeffrey Epstein|a long-standing association]] with the American financier and child sex offender [[Jeffrey Epstein]]. In 2014, [[Virginia Giuffre]] said that she had been [[Sex trafficking|sex trafficked]] to Andrew by Epstein and [[Ghislaine Maxwell]]. Andrew denied any wrongdoing, and in 2022 settled [[Giuffre v. Prince Andrew|a civil lawsuit]] with Giuffre in the United States without admission of liability. In the same year, Elizabeth II removed his military affiliations and patronages, and he ceased using the style "[[Royal Highness]]". In 2025, Charles III removed Andrew's remaining [[royal styles]] and [[Orders, decorations, and medals of the United Kingdom|honours]], and restricted his use of titles and [[Peerages in the United Kingdom|peerages]]. He vacated [[Royal Lodge]], part of the [[Crown Estate]], and relocated to private accommodation on the [[Sandringham estate]]. Following the release of more [[Epstein files]] in early 2026, Andrew was arrested on suspicion of [[misconduct in public office]] and was later released under investigation.

{{toc limit|3}}

==Early life==
During a 45-day tour of Canada in June and July 1959, [[Queen Elizabeth II]] discovered that she was pregnant. After her return to London,<ref>Multiple sources:
*{{cite news|last1=Rezaei |first1=Asal |title=Historian recounts Queen Elizabeth II's 1959 visit to Chicago |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/historian-recounts-queen-elizabeth-iis-1959-visit-to-chicago |website=CBS News Chicago |access-date=25 July 2025 |date=8 September 2022 |archive-date=24 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250824220739/https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/historian-recounts-queen-elizabeth-iis-1959-visit-to-chicago/ |url-status=live }}
*{{cite news|title=Secrets, Morning Sickness and an Assassination Attempt: The Bumpy History of Royal Pregnancy |url=https://www.eonline.com/ca/news/980737/secrets-morning-sickness-and-an-assassination-attempt-the-bumpy-history-of-royal-pregnancy |website=E! Online |access-date=26 July 2025 |date=26 October 2018 |archive-date=6 September 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250906044642/https://www.eonline.com/ca/news/980737/secrets-morning-sickness-and-an-assassination-attempt-the-bumpy-history-of-royal-pregnancy |url-status=live }}
*{{cite news|last1=Davison |first1=Janet |title=How royal pregnancy lost its privacy |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/how-royal-pregnancy-lost-its-privacy-1.1159146 |website=CBC |access-date=26 July 2025 |date=4 December 2012 |archive-date=26 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250826182818/https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/how-royal-pregnancy-lost-its-privacy-1.1159146 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Buckingham Palace]] announced on 7 August that she would not undertake further public engagements, a customary indication of royal pregnancy.<ref>{{cite news |date=8 August 1959 |title=Elizabeth II Is Expecting Third Child Next Winter |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1959/08/08/archives/elizabeth-ii-is-expecting-third-child-next-winter-queen-elizabeth.html |access-date=24 September 2025 |work=The New York Times}}</ref> Andrew was born a [[British prince|prince of the United Kingdom]] at 3:30{{nbsp}}pm on 19 February 1960 at Buckingham Palace,<ref>{{London Gazette |issue=41961 |date=20 February 1960 |page=1377 }}</ref> the third child and second son of Queen Elizabeth II and [[Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh]]. He was [[Christian name|christened]] Andrew Albert Christian Edward in the Music Room at the palace on 8 April.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rct.uk/collection/2153189/queen-elizabeth-ii-holding-an-infantnbspprince-andrew|title=Queen Elizabeth II holding an infant Prince Andrew|work=Royal Collection Trust|accessdate=17 December 2021|quote=The Queen's third child, Prince Andrew, was born on 19 February 1960 at Buckingham Palace, and was christened on 8 April.|archive-date=30 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241130174222/https://www.rct.uk/collection/2153189/queen-elizabeth-ii-holding-an-infantnbspprince-andrew|url-status=live}}</ref>

Andrew was the first child born to a reigning British monarch since [[Princess Beatrice of the United Kingdom|Princess Beatrice]], the youngest daughter of [[Queen Victoria]], in 1857.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-23272491|title=Royal Family tree and line of succession|work=BBC News|date=4 September 2017|access-date=14 March 2018|archive-date=11 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210311001051/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-23272491|url-status=live}}</ref> Like his siblings, [[Charles III|Charles]], [[Anne, Princess Royal|Anne]] and [[Prince Edward, Duke of Edinburgh|Edward]], he was looked after by a [[governess]], who oversaw his early education at Buckingham Palace.<ref name="BBC-2011">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-12663378|title=Prince Andrew: Envoy career plagued with controversy|work=BBC News|date=21 July 2011|access-date=14 March 2018|quote=Educated by a governess, then at Heatherdown Prep School, Surrey, and Gordonstoun in Scotland|archive-date=18 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210218211547/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-12663378|url-status=live}}</ref> He later attended [[Heatherdown School]] near [[Ascot, Berkshire|Ascot]] in [[Berkshire]].<ref name="Barkham-2004">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2004/jul/09/freedomofinformation.monarchy|title=The Guardian profile: Prince Andrew|newspaper=The Guardian|first=Patrick|last=Barkham|date=9 July 2004|access-date=14 March 2018|archive-date=16 March 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210316171307/https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2004/jul/09/freedomofinformation.monarchy|url-status=live}}</ref> In September 1973, he entered [[Gordonstoun]] in [[Moray]], which his father and elder brother had also attended.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.scotsman.com/education/gordonstoun-turns-back-clock-to-a-golden-age-of-cold-showers-but-would-prince-charles-agree-2443899 |title= Gordonstoun turns back clock to a golden age of cold showers (but would Prince Charles agree?) |author= <!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date= 28 April 2009 |work=The Scotsman|publisher= National World Limited |access-date=11 February 2026 |url-status=live|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260211022934/https://www.scotsman.com/education/gordonstoun-turns-back-clock-to-a-golden-age-of-cold-showers-but-would-prince-charles-agree-2443899 |archive-date= 11 February 2026 }}</ref> He was nicknamed "the Sniggerer" by his schoolmates at Gordonstoun, because of "his penchant for off-colour jokes, at which he laughed inordinately".<ref>{{cite book |last1=Cawthorne |first1=Nigel |title=Prince Andrew: Epstein, Maxwell and the Palace |date=2021 |publisher=Gibson Square |isbn=9781783341771}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=New book claims Prince Andrew was 'obsessed' with pornography |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/lifestyle/new-book-claims-prince-andrew-was-obsessed-with-pornography/WK6QRQVMQLBHKR5SQHNTRXIGOQ/ |access-date=1 May 2022 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |archive-date=16 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250516015726/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/lifestyle/new-book-claims-prince-andrew-was-obsessed-with-pornography/WK6QRQVMQLBHKR5SQHNTRXIGOQ/ |url-status=live }}</ref> While there, he spent six months{{snd}}from January to June 1977{{snd}}participating in an exchange programme at [[Lakefield College School]] in Canada.<ref name="Barkham-2004"/><ref name="DoY-Ed">{{cite web|url=https://thedukeofyork.org/about-the-duke/early-life-and-education/|title=Early Life & Education|date=26 October 2015|publisher=The Duke of York|access-date=14 March 2018|archive-date=4 May 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200504123603/https://thedukeofyork.org/about-the-duke/early-life-and-education/|url-status=live}}</ref> He left Gordonstoun in July two years later with [[Advanced Level (UK)|A-levels]]<ref name="DoY-Ed"/> in English, history, and economics.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/the-life-and-times-of-prince-andrew-born-to-be-spare-hgq5h96rv|title=The life and times of Prince Andrew|date=25 November 2019|newspaper=[[The Times]]|location=London, England|url-access=subscription|archive-date=27 May 2025|access-date=2 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527191348/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/the-life-and-times-of-prince-andrew-born-to-be-spare-hgq5h96rv|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Naval military service==

===Training===
The [[Royal Households of the United Kingdom|Royal Household]] announced in November 1978 that Andrew would join the [[Royal Navy]] the following year. In December, he underwent a series of sporting tests and examinations at the [[Officer and Aircrew Selection Centre|Aircrew Selection Centre]], at [[London Biggin Hill Airport|RAF Biggin Hill]], followed by further tests and interviews at [[RNAS Lee-on-Solent (HMS Daedalus)|HMS ''Daedalus'']] and the [[Admiralty Interview Board]], {{HMS|Sultan|1956 shore establishment|6}}. During March and April 1979, he was enrolled at the Royal Naval College Flight for pilot training, after which he was accepted as a trainee helicopter pilot and signed on for 12 years from 11 May 1979. On 1 September that year, Andrew was appointed a [[midshipman]] and entered [[Britannia Royal Naval College]], Dartmouth. He also completed the [[Royal Marines]] [[All Arms Commando Course]] in 1979, for which he received his [[green beret]].<ref name=naval/> He was commissioned as a [[sub-lieutenant]] on 1 September 1981 and appointed to the Trained Strength on 22 October.<ref>{{London Gazette|issue=49322|supp=y|page=5304|date=19 April 1983}}</ref>

After [[Passing out (military)|passing out]] from Dartmouth, Andrew undertook elementary flying training with the [[Royal Air Force]] at [[RAF Leeming]], followed by basic flying training with the navy at [[RNAS Culdrose (HMS Seahawk)|HMS ''Seahawk'']], where he learned to fly the [[Aérospatiale Gazelle|Gazelle]] helicopter.<ref name=naval/> After being awarded his wings, he progressed to advanced training on the [[Westland Sea King|Sea King]] helicopter and carried out operational flying training until 1982. He subsequently joined [[820 Naval Air Squadron]] on the [[aircraft carrier]] {{HMS|Invincible|R05|6}}.<ref name=naval/>

=== Falklands War ===
On 2 April 1982, Argentina [[1982 invasion of the Falkland Islands|invaded the Falkland Islands]], a [[British Overseas Territory]] administered by the United Kingdom and claimed by Argentina, triggering the [[Falklands War]].<ref>{{cite book|first1=Charles|last1=Cawley|title=Colonies in Conflict: The History of the British Overseas Territories|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TXnWCgAAQBAJ&dq=Argentina+invaded+the+Falkland+Islands,+a+British+overseas+territory+claimed+by+it+on+2+April+1982,+leading+to+the+Falklands+War&pg=PA202|publisher=Cambridge Scholars Publishing|date=2015|isbn=978-1-4438-8128-9|page=202}}</ref> ''Invincible'' was one of only two operational aircraft carriers available to the Royal Navy and therefore played a major role in the [[Falklands War order of battle: British naval forces|task force]] assembled to retake the islands.<ref>{{cite book|first1=Glen|last1=O'Hara|title=Britain and the Sea: Since 1600|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=FgwdBQAAQBAJ&dq=Hermes+invincible+task+force+available&pg=PA213|publisher=Macmillan International Higher Education|date=2010|isbn=978-1-137-07312-9|pages=213–214}}{{Dead link|date=March 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

Andrew's presence on board, and the risk of a royal family member being killed in action, made the British government apprehensive, and the [[Cabinet of the United Kingdom|Cabinet]] sought to move him to a desk role for the duration of the conflict. The Queen, however, insisted that her son remain with his ship.<ref>{{cite book|title=Queen Elizabeth II and the Royal Family: A Glorious Illustrated History|year= 2015 |publisher=[[DK Publishing]]|location=London|isbn=978-1465438003|page=217}}</ref> Andrew served on ''Invincible'' as a Sea King helicopter co-pilot, flying missions that included [[Anti-submarine warfare|anti-submarine]] and [[anti-surface warfare]], [[Exocet missile]] decoy operations, casualty evacuation, transport, and search and air rescue.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://en.mercopress.com/2001/06/12/prince-andrew-talks-of-his-dangerous-falklands-experiences|title=Prince Andrew Talks of His Dangerous Falklands Experiences|work=MercoPress|date=12 June 2001|archive-date=6 August 2020|access-date=12 January 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806202944/https://en.mercopress.com/2001/06/12/prince-andrew-talks-of-his-dangerous-falklands-experiences|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1982/06/19/Prince-Andrew-a-hero-of-the-Falklands-war/3976393307200/|title=Prince Andrew, a hero of the Falklands war|website=[[United Press International|UPI]]|date=19 June 1982|archive-date=16 March 2021|access-date=12 January 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210316171839/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1982/06/19/Prince-Andrew-a-hero-of-the-Falklands-war/3976393307200/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1982/06/03/Helicopter-pilot-Prince-Andrew-is-flying-anti-submarine-patrols-in/4652391924800/|title=Helicopter pilot Prince Andrew is flying anti-submarine patrols in...|work=[[United Press International|UPI]]|date=3 June 1982|archive-date=6 August 2020|access-date=12 January 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806154809/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1982/06/03/Helicopter-pilot-Prince-Andrew-is-flying-anti-submarine-patrols-in/4652391924800/|url-status=live}}</ref> He witnessed the Argentine attack on {{SS|Atlantic Conveyor}}.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=NPs9AAAAIBAJ&pg=4110,2766700|title=Prince Andrew talks of Falklands horror|work=Glasgow Herald|date=14 November 1983|page=2|archive-date=22 January 2020|access-date=21 September 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200122212127/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=NPs9AAAAIBAJ&pg=4110,2766700|url-status=live}}</ref>

At the end of the war, ''Invincible'' returned to [[Portsmouth]], where Elizabeth and Philip joined other families of the crew in welcoming the vessel home. According to historian [[Andrew Lownie]], the [[National Reorganization Process|Argentine military government]] planned, but ultimately did not attempt, to assassinate Andrew on [[Mustique]] in July 1982.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Argentine navy plotted to assassinate Prince Andrew within... |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1983/10/16/The-Argentine-navy-plotted-to-assassinate-Prince-Andrew-within/3229435124800/ |date=16 October 1983 |access-date=2026-03-01 |website=UPI |language=en}}</ref> Although he had brief assignments to {{HMS|Illustrious|R06|6}}, [[RNAS Culdrose]], and the [[School of Service Intelligence]], Andrew remained with ''Invincible'' until 1983. Commander [[Nigel Ward]]'s memoir ''Sea Harrier Over the Falklands'' described Andrew as "an excellent pilot and a very promising officer."<ref>{{cite book|last1=Ward|first1=Sharky|title=Sea Harrier Over the Falklands|year=1993|publisher=[[Pen and Sword Books]]|location=Barnsley, South Yorkshire|isbn=978-0850523058|page=113|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Dt7RAwAAQBAJ |access-date=12 September 2016}}</ref>

===Career officer===
[[File:Prince Andrew and Leon E. Panetta.jpg|thumb|With the US secretary of defence [[Leon Panetta]] commemorating the 100th anniversary of Naval Aviation at the [[National Building Museum]] in 2011]]

In late 1983, Andrew transferred to [[RNAS Portland]] and was trained to fly the [[Westland Lynx|Lynx]] helicopter.<ref name=naval/> On 1 February 1984, he was promoted to lieutenant,<ref>{{London Gazette|issue=49633|supp=y|page=1382|date=31 January 1984}}</ref> after which Elizabeth appointed him her [[personal aide-de-camp]].<ref>{{London Gazette|issue=49639|supp=y|page=1735|date=7 February 1984}}</ref> Andrew served aboard {{HMS|Brazen|F91|6}} as a flight pilot until 1986,<ref name=naval/> including deployment to the [[Mediterranean Sea]] as part of [[Standing NRF Maritime Group 2]]. He undertook the Lieutenants' [[Royal Naval College, Greenwich|Greenwich]] Staff course.<ref name=naval/>

On 23 October 1986, he transferred to the [[General List]] and enrolled on a four-month helicopter warfare instructor's course at [[RNAS Yeovilton]]. Upon graduation, he served from February 1987 to April 1988 as a helicopter warfare officer in [[702 Naval Air Squadron]], [[RNAS Portland]]. He later served on {{HMS|Edinburgh|D97|6}} as [[Watchstanding|officer of the watch]] and Assistant Navigating Officer until 1989, including a six-month deployment to the Far East as part of exercise ''Outback 88''.<ref name=naval/>

From 1989 to 1991, Andrew served as flight commander and pilot of the Lynx HAS3 on {{HMS|Campbeltown|F86|6}}. He also acted as force aviation officer to [[Standing NRF Maritime Group 1]] while ''Campbeltown'' was flagship of the [[NATO]] force in the [[Atlantic Ocean|North Atlantic]] from 1990 to 1991.<ref name=naval/> He passed the squadron command examination on 16 July 1991, attended the [[Staff College, Camberley]] in 1992, and completed the Army Staff course. He was promoted to [[Lieutenant commander (Royal Navy)|lieutenant-commander]] on 1 February and passed the ship command examination on 12 March 1992. From 1993 to 1994 Andrew commanded the Hunt-class [[minehunter]] {{HMS|Cottesmore|M32|6}}.<ref name=naval/>

From 1995 to 1996, Andrew was posted as senior pilot of [[815 Naval Air Squadron]], then the largest flying unit in the [[Fleet Air Arm]]. His main responsibility was to supervise flying standards and ensure effective operational capability.<ref name=naval/> He was promoted to [[Commander (Royal Navy)|commander]] on 27 April 1999.<ref name=naval/> He concluded his active naval career at the [[Ministry of Defence (United Kingdom)|Ministry of Defence]] in 2001 as an officer of the Diplomatic Directorate of the Naval Staff.<ref name=naval/> In July that year, Andrew was retired from the [[Navy Directory|Active List of the Navy]].<ref name=LG56295/> He was made an honorary [[Captain (Royal Navy)|captain]] in 2004,<ref name=LG57705/> promoted to [[Rear Admiral (Royal Navy)|rear admiral]] on his 50th birthday on 19 February 2010,<ref name=LG59341/> and to [[Vice Admiral (Royal Navy)|vice admiral]] in 2015.<ref name=LG61660/><ref>{{cite news|last=Low|first=Valentine|url=https://www.thetimes.co.uk/tto/news/uk/royalfamily/article4351422.ece|title=Queen makes Prince Andrew a vice-admiral|newspaper=[[The Times]]|location=London|date=12 February 2015|access-date=25 February 2015|url-access=subscription|archive-date=21 July 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160721113544/http://www.thetimes.co.uk/tto/news/uk/royalfamily/article4351422.ece|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Personal life==

===Relationships===
====Before marriage====
In May 1978, the ''[[The Evening News (London newspaper)|Evening News]]'' reported that Andrew had acquired the nickname "Randy Andy" (with "[[wikt:randy|randy]]" being British slang for "sexually eager") while at [[Gordonstoun]], owing to his being romantically involved with several women.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Miller |first=Compton |date=9 May 1978 |title=Andrew faces year of swotting for Cambridge |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0005765/19780509/076/0007 |url-access=registration |url-status=live |access-date=18 February 2026 |website=[[British Newspaper Archive]] |page= |postscript=. The article was part of Compton Miller's ''In Town'' column on page 7 of the 9 May 1978 ''[[The Evening News (London newspaper)|Evening News]]''.}}</ref> [[United Press International|UPI]] also used this nickname, stating that before beginning his naval career in 1979 he "seemed to be in training as a professional [[Playboy lifestyle|playboy]]".<ref>{{cite news |title=Prince Andrew given hero's welcome |url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1982/09/17/Prince-Andrew-given-heros-welcome/1053401083200/ |access-date=10 November 2025 |work=[[United Press International]] |date=17 April 1982}}</ref> Andrew met the American photographer and actress [[Koo Stark]] in February 1981, before his active service in the Falklands War.<ref name="Battersby-2015" /><ref>{{cite book|first=Alastair|last=Burnet|author-link=Alastair Burnet|title=The ITN Book of the Royal Wedding|publisher=[[Michael O'Mara Books]]|location=London, England|date=1986|page=38|quote=The actress Miss Koo Stark was a regular girlfriend of Prince Andrew for several years.}}</ref> In October 1982 they holidayed together on the island of [[Mustique]].<ref>{{cite book|first=Kim|last=McNamara|title=Paparazzi: Media Practices and Celebrity Culture|publisher=[[Polity (publisher)|Polity]]|location=Cambridge, England|date=2015|isbn=978-0745651743|page=29}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|first=Lady Colin|last=Campbell|author-link=Lady Colin Campbell|title=The Real Diana|publisher=[[Arcadia Publishing]]|location=Mount Pleasant, South Carolina|date=1998|asin=B01K562L9Q|page=161}}</ref> [[Tina Brown]] later described Stark as Andrew's only serious love interest.<ref name="Brown-2007">{{cite book|first=Tina|last=Brown|author-link=Tina Brown|title=The Diana Chronicles|publisher=[[Broadway Books]]|location=New York City|date=2007|isbn=978-0-7679-2309-5|page=228|title-link=The Diana Chronicles}}</ref> The couple separated in 1983 under pressure from the press and the palace.<ref name="Battersby-2015" /><ref name="Brown-2007" /> In 1997, Andrew became godfather to Stark's daughter.<ref>''[[Newsweek]]'', Volume 128 (1997), p. 76</ref> When Andrew faced accusations in 2015 regarding his association to Jeffrey Epstein, Stark publicly defended him.<ref name="Battersby-2015">{{cite news|first=Matilda|last=Battersby|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/news/koo-stark-speaks-out-to-help-rebut-prince-andrew-sex-slave-claims-10046948.html|title=Prince Andrew's ex Koo Stark speaks about their relationship for first time in 30 years|date=15 February 2015|work=[[The Independent]]|location=London, England|access-date=21 December 2016|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170121050247/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/news/koo-stark-speaks-out-to-help-rebut-prince-andrew-sex-slave-claims-10046948.html|archive-date=21 January 2017}}</ref>

====Marriage and children====
{{Further|Wedding of Prince Andrew and Sarah Ferguson|Descendants of Elizabeth II#Children of Andrew and Sarah}}
[[File:The Duke and Duchess of York in Townsville, 1988.jpg|thumb|left|Andrew and Sarah in [[Townsville]], Australia, 1988]]
Andrew married [[Sarah Ferguson]] at [[Westminster Abbey]] on 23 July 1986. On the same day, Elizabeth created him [[Duke of York]], [[Earl of Inverness]] and [[Baron Killyleagh]];<ref>{{London Gazette |issue=50606 |date=23 July 1986 |page=1 |supp=y}}</ref> the first two of these titles had previously been held by both his maternal grandfather, [[George VI]], and his great-grandfather [[George V]]. Andrew had known Ferguson since childhood; they had met occasionally at [[polo]] matches and became reacquainted at [[Royal Ascot]] in 1985.<ref name="DeYoung-1986">{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/lifestyle/1986/07/22/fergie-bedlam-over-the-bride/e0ddc0f5-6a78-444b-be1b-19b4494acf64/|title=Fergie: Bedlam Over the Bride|newspaper=The Washington Post|first=Karen|last=DeYoung|date=22 July 1986|access-date=9 March 2018|url-access=subscription|archive-date=20 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180620051038/https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/lifestyle/1986/07/22/fergie-bedlam-over-the-bride/e0ddc0f5-6a78-444b-be1b-19b4494acf64/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The couple initially appeared to have a happy marriage and had two daughters, [[Princess Beatrice]] (born 1988) and [[Princess Eugenie]] (born 1990), presenting a united public image during the late 1980s. Sarah's personal qualities were regarded as refreshing within the formal protocol of the royal family.<ref name="DeYoung-1986"/> Andrew's frequent travel due to his naval career, combined with relentless and often critical media attention on the Duchess of York, contributed to strains in the marriage.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Entertainment/sarah-ferguson-reveals-road-recovery-documentary-oprah-winfrey/story?id=13780411|title=Sarah Ferguson Reveals Her Road to Recovery in Documentary on OWN, Oprah Winfrey Network|publisher=ABC News|first=Katie|last=Kindelan|date=11 May 2011|access-date=6 December 2020|quote=She moved with her prince into Buckingham Palace but two weeks into their marriage, Mountbatten-Windsor was sent to sea and she only saw him for 40 days a year for the first five years.|archive-date=20 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250420203107/https://abcnews.go.com/Entertainment/sarah-ferguson-reveals-road-recovery-documentary-oprah-winfrey/story?id=13780411|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2002/oct/27/monarchy.paulharris|title=Fergie: How food became my only friend|newspaper=The Guardian|first=Paul|last=Harris|date=26 October 2002|access-date=25 November 2020|quote=The ex-wife of Prince Andrew described how she struggled against obesity for most of her life and was devastated when tabloids dubbed her 'Duchess of Pork' and 'Fat Fergie'.}}</ref>

The couple made a well publicised [[1988 royal tour of California|royal tour of California]] in March 1988.<ref name=UPIMar88>{{cite news|last=Crane|first=Alice|url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1988/03/07/The-duke-and-duchess-of-York-bid-cheerio-to/6861573714000/|archive-url=|title=The duke and duchess of York bid cheerio to...|date=7 March 1988|work=[[United Press International]]|accessdate=25 February 2026|archivedate=}}</ref>

On 19 March 1992, the couple announced plans to separate and did so amicably.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/march/19/newsid_2543000/2543667.stm|title=1992: Fergie and Andrew split|work=BBC News|access-date=15 March 2018|date=19 March 1992|archive-date=7 March 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080307130937/http://news.bbc.co.uk/onthisday/hi/dates/stories/march/19/newsid_2543000/2543667.stm|url-status=live}}</ref> That August, [[Tabloid journalism|tabloid]] newspapers published photographs of the businessman John Bryan sucking Sarah's toes, effectively ending any prospect of reconciliation. Throughout the separation, Ferguson had maintained that Bryan was her financial adviser, a claim Andrew accepted.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Elser |first1=Daniela |title=The toe-sucking photo that ruined the Duchess of York |url=https://www.news.com.au/entertainment/celebrity-life/royals/the-toesucking-photo-that-ruined-the-duchess-of-york/news-story/abd664af0ab8944a6e9cd4bdc1b57446 |website=news.com.au |access-date=19 June 2023 |date=29 July 2019 |archive-date=16 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220916170407/https://www.news.com.au/entertainment/celebrity-life/royals/the-toesucking-photo-that-ruined-the-duchess-of-york/news-story/abd664af0ab8944a6e9cd4bdc1b57446 |url-status=live }}</ref> The marriage ended in divorce on 30 May 1996. Andrew spoke warmly of his former wife in 2008, saying, "We have managed to work together to bring our children up in a way that few others have been able to and I am extremely grateful to be able to do that."<ref>{{Cite news| last=Castle| first=Stephen| title=From Prince Andrew, critical words for U.S. on Iraq| newspaper=The New York Times| date=4 February 2008| url=https://www.nytimes.com/2008/02/04/world/americas/04iht-andrew.4.9735778.html| url-access=limited| archive-date=12 September 2024| access-date=23 February 2017| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240912185550/https://www.nytimes.com/2008/02/04/world/americas/04iht-andrew.4.9735778.html| url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Prince Andrew and Princess Eugenie.JPG|thumb|Andrew and his daughter Eugenie riding in the carriage procession at [[Trooping the Colour]], 16 June 2012]]
In May 2010, Ferguson was filmed by a ''[[News of the World]]'' reporter stating that Andrew had agreed that, if she were to receive £500,000, he would meet the donor and provide useful top-level business contacts. She was filmed receiving US$40,000 in cash as a down-payment. The newspaper reported that Andrew had no knowledge of the arrangement.<ref>{{cite news|title=Duchess of York 'wanted cash for Prince Andrew access'|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/8699139.stm|work=BBC News|access-date=7 July 2011|date=23 May 2010|quote=The paper says the prince – a UK trade envoy – knew nothing about the deal|archive-date=25 August 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825062204/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/uk/8699139.stm|url-status=live}}</ref> In July 2011, Ferguson said that her multi-million-pound debts had been cleared through the intervention of her former husband, whom she described as a "knight on a white charger".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/8658857/Duchess-of-York-clears-debts-thanks-to-knight-on-white-charger-Andrew.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/8658857/Duchess-of-York-clears-debts-thanks-to-knight-on-white-charger-Andrew.html |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Duchess of York clears debts thanks to 'knight on white charger' Andrew|author=Collins, Nick|date=25 July 2011|work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London|access-date=15 December 2013}}{{cbignore}}</ref>

In 2011, Ferguson said that she had made a "gigantic error of judgement" in allowing Epstein to pay off a debt for her, and apologised for accepting money from him. She nevertheless continued to defend Andrew's former friendship with Epstein.<ref>{{Cite news |date=12 April 2012 |title=Duchess of York apologises for 'gigantic error of judgement' over debt |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/hp/front/duchess-of-york-apologises-for-gigantic-error-of-judgement-over-debt-6574623.html |access-date=19 February 2023 |website=Evening Standard |language=en |archive-date=14 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250614190510/https://www.standard.co.uk/hp/front/duchess-of-york-apologises-for-gigantic-error-of-judgement-over-debt-6574623.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=16 February 2022 |title=Prince Andrew: Where does he get his money from? |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-60401663 |access-date=19 February 2023 |archive-date=17 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250417154542/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-60401663 |url-status=live }}</ref> It later emerged that, following her public statement, she had sent an email to Epstein in which she referred to him as "a steadfast, generous and supreme friend".<ref>{{cite news |last=Coughlan |first=Sean |date=21 September 2025 |title=Sarah Ferguson dropped from multiple charities over Epstein email |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cddmr6v0jpzo |access-date=22 September 2025 |work=BBC News |archive-date=5 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251005103317/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cddmr6v0jpzo |url-status=live }}</ref>

====Post-divorce====
In 1999, Andrew was briefly in a relationship with [[Lady Victoria Hervey]], who has since made a number of statements in his support.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/lady-victoria-hervey-prince-andrew-who-b2006663.html|title=Lady Victoria Hervey: Prince Andrew's former girlfriend in profile|work=The Independent|first=Laurie|last=Churchman|date=15 February 2022|access-date=1 May 2025|archive-date=10 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250610014721/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/lady-victoria-hervey-prince-andrew-who-b2006663.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Residences===
As Andrew and Ferguson shared custody of their two daughters, the family continued to live at [[Sunninghill Park]], which had been built for the couple near [[Windsor Great Park]] in 1990, until Andrew moved to [[Royal Lodge]] in 2004. In 2007, Ferguson moved into Dolphin House in [[Englefield Green]], less than a mile from Royal Lodge.<ref name="Borland-2008">{{Cite news|last=Borland|first=Sophie|date=30 January 2008|title=Scented candle starts fire at Fergie's home |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1576995/Scented-candle-starts-fire-at-Fergies-home.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1576995/Scented-candle-starts-fire-at-Fergies-home.html |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription|url-status=live|access-date=21 September 2021|website=The Daily Telegraph}}{{cbignore}}</ref> A fire at Dolphin House in 2008<ref name="Borland-2008" /> led her to move into Royal Lodge, once again sharing a home with Andrew.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.ie/world-news/europe/fergie-and-andrew-will-definitely-remarry-friends-say-29525971.html|title=Fergie and Andrew will definitely remarry, friends say|work=Irish Independent|via=Telegraph|date=25 August 2013|accessdate=21 September 2021|archive-date=21 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210921142200/https://www.independent.ie/world-news/europe/fergie-and-andrew-will-definitely-remarry-friends-say-29525971.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Andrew's lease of Royal Lodge was for 75 years, held from the [[Crown Estate]], with a single £1 million premium and a commitment to spend £7.5 million on refurbishment.<ref>National Audit Office report, 2005. [https://www.nao.org.uk/report/the-crown-estate-property-leases-with-the-royal-family/ "The Crown Estate – Property Leases with the Royal Family"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220608034510/https://www.nao.org.uk/report/the-crown-estate-property-leases-with-the-royal-family/ |date=8 June 2022 }}.</ref> In March 2023, it was reported that Andrew had been offered [[Frogmore Cottage]] after his nephew [[Prince Harry]] was asked to vacate the residence.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://people.com/royals/meghan-markle-prince-harry-requested-vacate-frogmore-cottage-uk-home/|title=Meghan Markle and Prince Harry Told 'to Vacate' Their U.K. Home, Frogmore Cottage|work=People|first=Stephanie|last=Petit|date=1 March 2023|accessdate=1 March 2023|archive-date=29 June 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230629201804/https://people.com/royals/meghan-markle-prince-harry-requested-vacate-frogmore-cottage-uk-home/|url-status=live}}</ref> The offer came amid reports that Andrew could no longer afford the running costs of Royal Lodge, as he was due to lose his annual grant.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-64812549|title=Harry and Meghan residence Frogmore Cottage offered to Andrew – reports|website=BBC News|date=1 March 2023|access-date=1 March 2023|archive-date=10 September 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230910052526/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-64812549|url-status=live}}</ref> In June 2026, it was revealed in a [[National Audit Office (United Kingdom)|National Audit Office]] report that Andrew had received an undisclosed amount of rental income from sub‑letting three cottages on the Royal Lodge estate.<ref>{{cite news |last=Davies |first=Caroline |date=5 June 2026 |title=Andrew sublet three cottages while paying peppercorn rent to Crown Estate |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/jun/05/andrew-sublet-three-cottages-while-paying-peppercorn-rent-to-crown-estate |access-date=5 June 2026 |work=The Guardian}}</ref>

In October 2025, it was reported that Andrew had paid a [[peppercorn rent]] for the Royal Lodge lease in exchange for upfront payments totalling £8.5 million, with the agreement entitling him and his family to reside at the property until 2078.<ref>{{cite news| last=Witherow | first=Tom | title=Prince Andrew has not paid rent on Royal Lodge for two decades | website=The Times and The Sunday Times | date=21 October 2025 | url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/prince-andrew-rent-free-windsor-mansion-rbrgfjkrr | access-date=21 October 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| last=Ross | first=Alex | title=Prince Andrew has paid no rent on Royal Lodge for more than 20 years | website=The Independent | date=21 October 2025 | url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-royal-lodge-windsor-giuffre-b2848964.html | access-date=21 October 2025}}</ref> Later that month, Buckingham Palace announced that formal notice had been served to surrender the lease. The Crown Estate later clarified that Andrew "will not be owed any compensation for early surrender of the lease ... once dilapidations are taken into account".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/02/world/andrew-mountbatten-windsor-royal-lodge-intl|title=Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor denied six-figure compensation payout for leaving Royal Lodge, crown estate says|work=CNN|first=Caitlin|last=Danaher|date=2 December 2025|access-date=2 December 2025|archive-date=2 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251202182025/https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/02/world/andrew-mountbatten-windsor-royal-lodge-intl|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Underwood |first1=Mitya |title=Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor's new life in Sandringham exile with surprising twist |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/royals/andrew-mountbatten-windsors-new-life-36434154 |access-date=26 December 2025 |work=Daily Mirror |date=21 December 2025 |archive-date=22 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251222105549/https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/royals/andrew-mountbatten-windsors-new-life-36434154 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 2 February 2026, Andrew left Royal Lodge and moved temporarily to [[Wood Farm]] on the Sandringham estate while his future accommodation underwent renovation.<ref>{{cite news|title=Andrew moves out of Royal Lodge home after latest Epstein files|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8e5zgprgn1o|work=BBC News|date=3 February 2026|last=Relph|first=Daniela|access-date=4 February 2026|archive-date=3 February 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260203233828/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8e5zgprgn1o|url-status=live}}</ref> He relocated to [[Marsh Farm, Sandringham|Marsh Farm]] in April 2026.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Murphy |first1=Helen |title=Ex-Prince Andrew Moves into His New Marsh Farm Home at Sandringham After Reportedly 'Dragging His Feet' |url=https://people.com/ex-prince-andrew-moves-marsh-farm-home-report-11945304 |access-date=8 April 2026 |work=People |date=8 April 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Andrews daughters join Royal Family in Sandringham without their father on Christmas Day |url=https://news.sky.com/story/andrews-daughters-join-royal-family-in-sandringham-without-their-father-on-christmas-day-13487353 |access-date=26 December 2025 |work=Sky News |archive-date=25 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251225230509/https://news.sky.com/story/andrews-daughters-join-royal-family-in-sandringham-without-their-father-on-christmas-day-13487353 |url-status=live }}</ref> Both properties are located on the [[Sandringham estate]], which is privately owned by the King.<ref name="Nanji-2025">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cr7m4yjkp74o|title=Andrew stripped of 'prince' title and will move out of Royal Lodge|work=BBC News|first=Noor|last=Nanji|date=30 October 2025|access-date=30 October 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Hatton |first1=Ben |last2=Davies |first2=Maia |title=Where might Andrew live on the Sandringham estate? |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c201zvrpvw9o |website=BBC |access-date=14 December 2025 |date=31 October 2025 |archive-date=19 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251219222256/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c201zvrpvw9o |url-status=live }}</ref>  

Andrew is a keen [[skier]], and in 2014 he bought a [[chalet]] in [[Verbier]], Switzerland, for £13 million, jointly with his ex-wife.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-30759303|title=Prince Andrew and Sarah Ferguson buy luxury chalet|work=BBC News|date=10 January 2015|accessdate=28 January 2022|archive-date=20 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250620030214/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-30759303|url-status=live}}</ref> In May 2020, it was reported that they were in a legal dispute over the mortgage.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2020/may/08/prince-andrew-to-face-legal-case-over-reported-5m-ski-chalet-debt|title=Prince Andrew to face legal case over reported £5m ski chalet debt|newspaper=The Guardian|date=7 May 2020|access-date=9 May 2020}}</ref> To purchase the property, they had secured a loan of £13.25 million and were expected to pay £5 million in cash instalments which, with interest, totalled £6.8 million.<ref name="Bodkin-2022">{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/08/07/sarah-fergusons-5m-mayfair-mews-purchase-outrages-chalet-owner/|title=Sarah Ferguson's £5m Mayfair mews purchase 'outrages' chalet owner burned by the Yorks|work=The Telegraph|first=Henry|last=Bodkin|date=7 August 2022|accessdate=8 August 2022|url-access=subscription|archive-date=8 August 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220808011029/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/08/07/sarah-fergusons-5m-mayfair-mews-purchase-outrages-chalet-owner/|url-status=live}}</ref> Despite claims that the Queen would assist with the payment, a spokesperson for Andrew confirmed that she "will not be stepping in to settle the debt".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/world/europe/article/queen-will-not-pick-up-andrews-chalet-debt-zpphcdvmk?region=global%2Cglobal|title=Queen will not pick up chalet debt, says Prince Andrew|work=[[The Times]]|first=Nadeem|last=Badshah|date=1 June 2020|access-date=1 June 2020|url-access=subscription|archive-date=27 May 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527191738/https://www.thetimes.com/world/europe/article/queen-will-not-pick-up-andrews-chalet-debt-zpphcdvmk?region=global%2Cglobal|url-status=live}}</ref>

''[[The Times]]'' reported in September 2021 that Andrew and Ferguson had reached a legal agreement with the chalet's previous owner and would sell the property.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.vanityfair.com/style/2021/09/prince-andrew-and-sarah-ferguson-ski-chalet-sale|title=Prince Andrew and Sarah Ferguson Have Solved One Big Financial Woe|magazine=Vanity Fair|first1=Katie|last1=Nicholl|first2=Erin|last2=Vanderhoof|date=28 September 2021|accessdate=28 September 2021|archive-date=28 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210928174106/https://www.vanityfair.com/style/2021/09/prince-andrew-and-sarah-ferguson-ski-chalet-sale|url-status=live}}</ref> The owner agreed to accept £3.4&nbsp;million{{snd}}half of what she was owed{{snd}}after being informed that Andrew and Ferguson were experiencing financial difficulties.<ref name="Bodkin-2022" /> Proceeds from the sale were reportedly intended to contribute to Andrew's legal expenses in relation to the [[Giuffre v. Prince Andrew|civil lawsuit]] with Virginia Giuffre.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-trial-swiss-chalet-b1988575.html|title=Prince Andrew 'rushing through sale of £17m Swiss chalet' as legal bills spiral|work=[[The Independent]]|first=Chiara|last=Giordano|date=7 January 2022|accessdate=9 January 2022|archive-date=26 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426162100/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-trial-swiss-chalet-b1988575.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In June 2022, ''[[Le Temps]]'' reported that the chalet had been frozen because of a £1.6&nbsp;million debt Andrew owed to unnamed individuals. Law professor Nicolas Jeandin told the newspaper "A sale is in principle impossible, except with the agreement of the creditor."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.geo.tv/latest/421845-prince-andrew-branded-absolute-fool-over-16-million-swiss-debt|title=Prince Andrew branded 'absolute fool' over £1.6 million Swiss debt|work=Geo TV|date=11 June 2022|accessdate=11 June 2022|archive-date=8 September 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250908014653/https://www.geo.tv/latest/421845-prince-andrew-branded-absolute-fool-over-16-million-swiss-debt|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/06/10/absolute-fool-prince-andrew-embroiled-another-debt-battle-time/|title='Absolute fool': Prince Andrew embroiled in another debt battle, this time over £1.6m|work=The Telegraph|first=Victoria|last=Ward|date=11 June 2022|accessdate=11 June 2022|url-access=subscription|archive-date=25 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241225013540/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/06/10/absolute-fool-prince-andrew-embroiled-another-debt-battle-time/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Health===
On 2 June 2022, Andrew tested positive for [[COVID-19]], and it was announced that he would not attend the [[Platinum Jubilee National Service of Thanksgiving]] at [[St Paul's Cathedral]] on 3 June.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.sky.com/story/prince-andrew-tests-positive-for-covid-buckingham-palace-says-12626242|title=Prince Andrew tests positive for COVID, Buckingham Palace says|work=Sky News|first=Amar|last=Mehta|date=2 June 2022|accessdate=2 June 2022|archive-date=31 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251231200118/https://news.sky.com/story/prince-andrew-tests-positive-for-covid-buckingham-palace-says-12626242|url-status=live}}</ref> Andrew is a [[teetotaller]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Adams|first=Guy|title=Prince Andrew: The playboy prince|date=9 December 2006|work=[[The Independent]]|access-date=18 March 2024|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/prince-andrew-the-playboy-prince-427707.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100507003658/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/profiles/prince-andrew-the-playboy-prince-427707.html|archive-date=7 May 2010}}</ref>

===Interests===
Andrew is a keen golfer and has held a low single-figure [[Handicap (golf)|handicap]].<ref>''Royal'', by Robert Lacey, 2002.</ref> He served as captain of [[the Royal and Ancient Golf Club of St Andrews]] between 2003 and 2004{{snd}}during the club's 250th anniversary season{{snd}}was patron of several royal golf clubs, and had been elected an honorary member of many others. In 2004, he was criticised by the [[Labour and Co-operative]] MP [[Ian Davidson (British politician)|Ian Davidson]], who, in a letter to the [[National Audit Office (United Kingdom)|National Audit Office]], questioned Andrew's decision to fly to St Andrews on RAF aircraft for two golfing trips.<ref name="Barkham-2004"/> Andrew resigned his honorary membership of the Royal and Ancient Golf Club of St Andrews after the Queen removed royal patronages at several golf clubs.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-60170912|title=Prince Andrew gives up honorary membership at prestigious golf club|work=BBC|first=Sean|last=Coughlan|date=28 January 2022|accessdate=28 January 2022|archive-date=25 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241225205735/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-60170912|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2022/jan/28/relief-as-prince-andrew-relinquishes-membership-of-royal-ancient-club|title=Relief as Prince Andrew relinquishes membership of Royal & Ancient Club|work=[[The Guardian]]|first=Ewan|last=Murray|date=28 January 2022|accessdate=28 January 2022}}</ref> His honorary membership of the [[Royal Dornoch Golf Club]] was revoked the following month.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.geo.tv/latest/401681-new-humiliation-for-prince-andrew-as-his-membership-of-royal-golf-club-revoked|title='New humiliation' for Prince Andrew as his membership of royal golf club revoked|work=Geo TV|date=26 February 2022|accessdate=1 March 2022|archive-date=26 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426142426/https://www.geo.tv/latest/401681-new-humiliation-for-prince-andrew-as-his-membership-of-royal-golf-club-revoked|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Charitable work==
===Patronages===
[[File:Dmitry Medvedev in Yuzhno-Sakhalink 18 February 2009-2.jpg|thumb|left|Andrew, Russian president [[Dmitry Medvedev]], Japanese prime minister [[Tarō Asō]], and Minister of Economic Affairs of The Netherlands [[Maria van der Hoeven]] visit the [[Sakhalin-II]] oil and gas project in the [[Russian Far East]], 2009]]
Andrew was [[patron]] of the Middle East Association (MEA), the UK's premier organisation for promoting trade and good relations with the Middle East, North Africa, Turkey, and Iran.<ref>{{cite web|title=The Middle East Association|url=http://www.english.globalarabnetwork.com/Organization-Profile/the-middle-east-association-london.html|work=Global Arab Network|access-date=7 January 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090803174139/http://www.english.globalarabnetwork.com/Organization-Profile/the-middle-east-association-london.html|archive-date=3 August 2009}}</ref> After his role as [[Special Representative for International Trade and Investment]] ended, he continued to support UK enterprise without holding a formal position. [[Robert Jobson]] wrote that Andrew carried out this working effectively, noting that "He is particularly passionate when dealing with young start-up entrepreneurs and bringing them together with successful businesses at networking and showcasing events. Andrew is direct and to the point, and his methods seem to work".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2015/01/23/opinion/prince-andrew-point/|title=What's the point of Prince Andrew?|author=Robert Jobson|date=23 January 2015|work=CNN|access-date=19 April 2016|archive-date=30 November 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241130174511/https://edition.cnn.com/2015/01/23/opinion/prince-andrew-point/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Andrew was patron of [[Fight for Sight (UK)|Fight for Sight]], a charity dedicated to research into the prevention and treatment of blindness and eye disease,<ref>[http://fightforsight.org.uk/324/articles/president-1327 "Message from the Royal Patron"] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150204054251/http://fightforsight.org.uk/324/articles/president-1327 |date=4 February 2015 }}, Fight for Sight, accessed 4 February 2015</ref> and was a member of [[the Scout Association]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Royal Support for the Scouting and Guiding Movements |publisher=Official Website of the British Monarchy |url=http://www.royal.gov.uk/output/Page5951.asp |access-date=25 July 2008 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090124230434/http://www.royal.gov.uk/output/Page5951.asp |archive-date=24 January 2009}}</ref> Andrew toured Canada frequently to undertake duties related to his Canadian military role. Rick Peters, former commanding officer of [[the Royal Highland Fusiliers of Canada]], stated that Andrew was "very well informed on Canadian military methods".<ref>Hurst, Jeff; [http://www.cambridgetimes.ca/cam/news/news_777717.html "Princely plans for Andrew"], ''Cambridge Times'' (Canada), 1 May 2007. {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071016200458/http://www.cambridgetimes.ca/cam/news/news_777717.html |date=16 October 2007 }}</ref> He became patron of the charity Attend<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.attend.org.uk/about-us/people-we-honour-0/attend-vips|title=Attend VIPs|access-date=19 April 2016|archive-date=30 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251230043442/https://www.attend.org.uk/about-us/people-we-honour-0/attend-vips|url-status=live}}</ref> in 2003 and served as a member of the International Advisory Board of the [[Royal United Services Institute]]. On 3 September 2012, Andrew was among a team of 40 people who abseiled down [[The Shard]], then the tallest building in Europe, to raise money for the educational charities the [[Outward Bound Trust]] and the Royal Marines Charitable Trust Fund.<ref>{{cite news|title=Prince Andrew rappels down U.K. building for charity|url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/prince-andrew-rappels-down-u-k-building-for-charity-1.1295400|work=CBC News|date=3 September 2012|access-date=7 September 2012|archive-date=12 September 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240912182949/https://www.cbc.ca/news/world/prince-andrew-rappels-down-u-k-building-for-charity-1.1295400|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Prince Andrew descends Europe's tallest building|url=http://www.cbsnews.com/video/watch/?id=7420354n|work=cbsnews|date=3 September 2012|access-date=7 September 2012|archive-date=6 September 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120906191119/http://www.cbsnews.com/video/watch/?id=7420354n|url-status=live}}</ref> He also supported organisations focused on science and technology, becoming patron of [[Catalyst Inc]] and [[TeenTech]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gov.uk/government/news/duke-of-york-visits-northern-ireland-science-park|title=Duke of York visits Northern Ireland Science Park|publisher=Gov.uk|date=29 January 2013|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=6 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206032457/https://www.gov.uk/government/news/duke-of-york-visits-northern-ireland-science-park|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/business/news/prince-andrew-launches-tech-park-offices-35138211.html|title=Prince Andrew launches tech park offices|newspaper=Belfast Telegraph|first=John|last=Mulgrew|date=18 October 2016|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=21 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220921225950/https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/business/news/prince-andrew-launches-tech-park-offices-35138211.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.teentech.com/teentechbuckingham-palace/|title=TeenTech@Buckingham Palace|publisher=TeenTech|first=Maggie|last=Philbin|date=12 November 2017|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=26 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180526191120/http://www.teentech.com/teentechbuckingham-palace/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In 2014, Andrew visited [[Geneva]], Switzerland, to promote British science at [[CERN]]'s 60th anniversary celebrations.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gov.uk/government/news/visit-of-his-royal-highness-the-duke-of-york-kg-to-geneva-switzerland-on-29-september-2014|title=Visit of His Royal Highness, The Duke of York, KG to Geneva, Switzerland|publisher=Gov.uk|date=1 September 2014|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=25 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241225171043/https://www.gov.uk/government/news/visit-of-his-royal-highness-the-duke-of-york-kg-to-geneva-switzerland-on-29-september-2014|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Duke of York - World Economic Forum on the Middle East 2008.jpg|thumb|Andrew in his role as the UK's Special Representative for International Trade and Investment at the [[World Economic Forum]] on the Middle East, 2008]]

In 2013, it was announced that Andrew would become patron of [[London Metropolitan University]]<ref>{{cite news|title=By Royal Appointment: London Met welcomes new Patron|url=https://www.londonmet.ac.uk/news/news-stories/university-news-2013/university-news-june/by-royal-appointment-/|publisher=London Metropolitan University|date=3 June 2013|archive-date=4 March 2016|access-date=4 September 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304053701/http://www.londonmet.ac.uk/news/news-stories/university-news-2013/university-news-june/by-royal-appointment-/|url-status=live}}</ref> and the [[University of Huddersfield]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Duke of York becomes University Patron|url=http://www.hud.ac.uk/business/news/dukeofyorkbecomesuniversitypatron.php|publisher=University of Huddersfield|date=2 July 2013|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150122054810/http://www.hud.ac.uk/business/news/dukeofyorkbecomesuniversitypatron.php|archive-date=22 January 2015}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.itv.com/news/calendar/2013-07-02/the-duke-of-york-to-be-patron-of-the-university-of-huddersfield/|title=The Duke of York to be Patron of the University of Huddersfield|work=ITV News|date=2 July 2013|archive-date=27 September 2024|access-date=2 July 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240927190827/https://www.itv.com/news/calendar/2013-07-02/the-duke-of-york-to-be-patron-of-the-university-of-huddersfield|url-status=live}}</ref> In July 2015, he was installed as Chancellor of the University of Huddersfield.<ref name="Huddersfield">{{cite news|title=HRH The Duke of York installed as University Chancellor|url=http://www.hud.ac.uk/news/2015/july/hrhthedukeofyorkinstalledasuniversitychancellor.php|publisher=University of Huddersfield|access-date=15 July 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150714171752/http://www.hud.ac.uk/news/2015/july/hrhthedukeofyorkinstalledasuniversitychancellor.php|archive-date=14 July 2015}}</ref> In recognition of his promotion of entrepreneurship, he was elected to an Honorary Fellowship at [[Hughes Hall]], in the [[University of Cambridge]] on 1 May 2018.<ref name="Hughes">{{cite web|url=https://www.hughes.cam.ac.uk/hrh-thedukeofyork-010518/|title=Hughes Hall appoints the Duke of York as an Honorary Fellow and HRH opens Gresham Court|date=1 May 2018|access-date=1 May 2018|archive-date=5 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210505180023/https://www.hughes.cam.ac.uk/hrh-thedukeofyork-010518/|url-status=dead}}</ref> On 19 November 2019, the [[National Union of Students (United Kingdom)|Students' Union]] of the University of Huddersfield passed a motion to lobby Andrew to resign as its chancellor, while London Metropolitan University was also reviewing his role as patron.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.itv.com/news/2019-11-19/huddersfield-university-students-vote-for-prince-andrew-to-quit-as-chancellor/|title=Huddersfield and London Met universities reviewing Prince Andrew's role following interview|website=ITV News|date=19 November 2019|access-date=19 November 2019|archive-date=25 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241225192945/https://www.itv.com/news/2019-11-19/huddersfield-university-students-vote-for-prince-andrew-to-quit-as-chancellor|url-status=live}}</ref> On 21 November, Andrew relinquished his role as Chancellor of the University of Huddersfield.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-leeds-50507521|title=Prince Andrew quits as University of Huddersfield chancellor|work=BBC News|date=21 November 2019|access-date=15 January 2020|archive-date=5 May 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250505030415/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-leeds-50507521|url-status=live}}</ref>

In March 2019, Andrew took over the patronage of the Outward Bound Trust from his father, the Duke of Edinburgh, serving until his own resignation in November 2019.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.sky.com/story/prince-andrew-could-be-handed-a-subpoena-to-face-epstein-questions-11866392|title=Prince Andrew could be handed a summons to face Epstein questions|website=Sky News|access-date=29 November 2019|archive-date=25 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241225194912/https://news.sky.com/story/prince-andrew-could-be-handed-a-subpoena-to-face-epstein-questions-11866392|url-status=live}}</ref> Andrew had been chairman of the organisation's board of trustees since 1999.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2019/03/12/prince-philip-passes-outward-bound-trust-patronage-prince-andrew/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2019/03/12/prince-philip-passes-outward-bound-trust-patronage-prince-andrew/ |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Prince Philip passes Outward Bound Trust patronage to Prince Andrew after interviewing him for the job|newspaper=The Telegraph|first=Hannah|last=Furness|date=12 March 2019|access-date=12 March 2019}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.outwardbound.org.uk/about-us/the-duke-of-york,-kg/|title=The Duke of York, KG|publisher=The Outward Bound Trust|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=26 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180526191155/https://www.outwardbound.org.uk/about-us/the-duke-of-york,-kg/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In May 2019, it was announced that Andrew had succeeded [[Lord Carrington]] as patron of the Royal Fine Art Commission Trust.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.architectsjournal.co.uk/news/prince-andrew-takes-design-champion-role/10042571.article|title=Prince Andrew takes design champion role|publisher=Architects Journal|first=Greg|last=Pitcher|date=15 May 2019|access-date=16 May 2019|archive-date=6 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200806120952/https://www.architectsjournal.co.uk/news/prince-andrew-takes-design-champion-role/10042571.article|url-status=live}}</ref> On 13 January 2022, it was announced that his royal patronages had been returned to the Queen to be redistributed among other members of the royal family.<ref name="BBC News-2022a">{{Cite news |date=13 January 2022 |title=Prince Andrew loses military titles and patronages |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-59987935 |access-date=13 January 2022 |work=BBC News |language=en-GB |archive-date=21 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220621122310/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-59987935 |url-status=live }}</ref> In January 2023, it was reported that King [[Charles III]] had agreed that Andrew could pursue some business interests.<ref name="Ward-2023">{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2023/01/21/late-queen-gave-prince-andrew-blessing-use-hrh-title/|title=Late Queen gave Prince Andrew blessing to use HRH title|work=The Telegraph|first=Victoria|last=Ward|date=21 January 2023|accessdate=21 January 2023|url-access=subscription|archive-date=16 February 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250216194103/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2023/01/21/late-queen-gave-prince-andrew-blessing-use-hrh-title/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In July 2025, the philanthropy adviser Giving Evidence published research examining the impact of Andrew's charity patronages on the incomes of the organisations he supported prior to his retirement from public duties. The study found that revenues at roughly half of the 35 registered charities in England for which Andrew had been the sole royal patron rose after his patronage ended, while revenues at the other half fell. Researchers then compared the 35 charities with others across the country and found "no material differences in revenue patterns when Andrew's patronages ceased". This and earlier studies supported the conclusion that having a royal patron did not significantly affect charities' incomes.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.civilsociety.co.uk/news/prince-andrew-patronages-did-not-benefit-charities-incomes-research-finds.html#sthash.5C85WDua.dpuf|title=Prince Andrew's patronages did not benefit charities' incomes, research finds|last=Moss|first=Emily|date=15 July 2025|work=Civil Society|accessdate=15 July 2025|archive-date=8 October 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251008153829/https://www.civilsociety.co.uk/news/prince-andrew-patronages-did-not-benefit-charities-incomes-research-finds.html#sthash.5C85WDua.dpuf|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Initiatives===
[[File:Hillary Rodham Clinton Prince Andrew Chatham House Prize 2013 Award Ceremony (10224154325).jpg|thumb|[[Hillary Clinton]] being presented with the 2013 [[Chatham House Prize]] by Andrew]]
[[File:President Trump at St. James's Palace (48000863521).jpg|thumb|With US President [[Donald Trump]] leaving [[Clarence House]] in London, June 2019]]
While touring India as part of the [[Diamond Jubilee of Elizabeth II|Queen's Diamond Jubilee]] in 2012,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/the_queens_diamond_jubilee/8955326/Royal-family-global-tour-to-mark-Diamond-Jubilee.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/the_queens_diamond_jubilee/8955326/Royal-family-global-tour-to-mark-Diamond-Jubilee.html |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Royal family global tour to mark Diamond Jubilee|newspaper=The Daily Telegraph|date=14 December 2011|access-date=26 May 2018}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Andrew became interested in the work of Women's Interlink Foundation (WIF), a charity that helps women acquire skills to earn an income. He and his family later initiated Key to Freedom, a project intended to "find a route to market for products made by WIF".<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://people.com/royals/prince-andrew-and-ex-wife-fergie-come-together-for-a-fashionable-cause/|title=Prince Andrew and Ex-Wife Fergie Come Together for a Fashionable Cause: 'It Is About Family Unity'|magazine=People|first=Simon|last=Perry|date=28 July 2015|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=3 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250603002243/https://people.com/royals/prince-andrew-and-ex-wife-fergie-come-together-for-a-fashionable-cause/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://keytofreedom.org/|title=Key to Freedom|publisher=Key to Freedom|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=6 February 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206065048/http://keytofreedom.org/|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2014, Andrew founded the Pitch@Palace initiative<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.royal.uk/duke-york-and-pitchpalace|title=The Duke of York and Pitch@Palace|author=Emma Goodey|date=4 April 2016|work=The Royal Family|access-date=1 November 2017|archive-date=12 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412083355/https://www.royal.uk/duke-york-and-pitchpalace|url-status=live}}</ref> to support entrepreneurs by amplifying and accelerating their business ideas. Entrepreneurs selected for Pitch@Palace Bootcamp were officially invited by Andrew<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nickhatter.com/single-post/2015/03/19/How-to-get-a-royal-invitation|title=How to get a royal invitation|website=Nick Hatter – Life Coach in London|access-date=1 November 2017|archive-date=31 May 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250531110745/https://www.nickhatter.com/single-post/2015/03/19/How-to-get-a-royal-invitation|url-status=live}}</ref> to attend [[St James's Palace]] to pitch their ideas and connect with potential investors, mentors, and business contacts.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://pitchatpalace.com/what-is-pitch-at-the-palace/|title=What is Pitch@Palace?|date=1 June 2017|work=Pitch@Palace|access-date=1 November 2017|archive-date=7 November 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171107065048/http://pitchatpalace.com/what-is-pitch-at-the-palace/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In May 2018, he visited China and opened the Pitch@Palace China Bootcamp 2.0 at [[Peking University]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.royal.uk/duke-york-visits-china-2018|title=The Duke of York visits China 2018|publisher=The Royal Family|date=1 June 2018|access-date=2 November 2018|archive-date=22 January 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250122175226/https://www.royal.uk/duke-york-visits-china-2018|url-status=live}}</ref> On 18 November 2019, accountancy firm [[KPMG]] announced it would not renew its sponsorship of Pitch@Palace,<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/nov/18/kpmg-ends-its-backing-for-prince-andrews-mentorship-scheme|title=KPMG ends its backing for Prince Andrew's mentorship scheme|first1=Haroon|last1=Siddique|first2=Anugraha|last2=Sundaravelu|date=18 November 2019|newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref> and on 19 November [[Standard Chartered]] also withdrew its support.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/nov/19/prince-andrew-student-body-calls-for-royal-to-step-down-huddersfield-university-as-chancellor|title=Prince Andrew mentor scheme at risk as firms withdraw support|first1=Ben|last1=Quinn|first2=Jim|last2=Waterson|first3=Jedidajah|last3=Otte|date=19 November 2019|newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref>

[[Amanda Thirsk]] became chief executive of Pitch@Palace in November 2019, following Andrew's withdrawal from royal duties.<ref name="bbcpatp">{{cite news|author1=Ben King |title=Prince Andrew's firm linked to controversial PPE millionaire |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9vep0p877wo |website=[[BBC News]] |access-date=13 February 2026 |date=29 April 2025 |archive-date=26 July 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250726103533/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9vep0p877wo |url-status=live }}</ref> Thirsk resigned as chief executive of Pitch@Palace in April 2020,<ref>{{cite news|title=Prince Andrew's loyal aide resigns from his flagship project as the Duke also steps back |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2020/04/07/prince-andrews-loyal-aide-resigns-flagship-project-duke-also/ |website=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=15 February 2026 |date=7 April 2020 |archive-date=19 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251119161010/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2020/04/07/prince-andrews-loyal-aide-resigns-flagship-project-duke-also/ |url-status=live }}{{subscription required}}</ref> and by early 2021, ownership was transferred to Knox House Trustees (UK) Limited, a company ultimately owned by [[Doug Barrowman]].<ref name="bbcpatp"/> In 2023, ownership of Knox House Trustees (UK) Limited was transferred to Andrew's accountant Arthur Lancaster.<ref name="bbcpatp"/> In November 2025, Pitch@Palace Global entered the dissolution process, initiated by its director, following the winding-up of its UK arm in 2021.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/nov/11/mountbatten-windsor-applies-to-shut-down-business-interests|title=Andrew Mountbatten Windsor applies to shut down business interests|work=The Guardian|first=Nadeem|last=Badshah|date=11 November 2025|access-date=13 November 2025}}</ref>

Andrew founded the Prince Andrew Charitable Trust which aimed to support young people in different areas such as education and training.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://beta.charitycommission.gov.uk/charity-details/?regid=290140&subid=0|title=The Prince Andrew Charitable Trust|publisher=Public register for charities (BETA)|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=7 August 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200807070703/https://beta.charitycommission.gov.uk/charity-details/?regid=290140&subid=0|url-status=live}}</ref> In May 2020, it was reported that the trust was under investigation by the [[Charity Commission]] regarding regulatory concerns about £350,000 in payments to his former private secretary [[Amanda Thirsk]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/prince-andrew-charitable-trust-to-be-wound-up-amid-charity-commission-investigation-qz6pzkx2z|title=Prince Andrew Charitable Trust to be wound up amid Charity Commission investigation|work=The Sunday Times|first=Roya|last=Nikkhah|date=10 May 2020|access-date=10 May 2020|url-access=subscription|archive-date=27 May 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527191329/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/prince-andrew-charitable-trust-to-be-wound-up-amid-charity-commission-investigation-qz6pzkx2z|url-status=live}}</ref> He also established several awards, including the Inspiring Digital Enterprise Award (iDEA), a programme designed to develop digital and enterprise skills,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hud.ac.uk/news/interviews/dukeofyorksinspiringdigitalenterpriseawards/|title=Duke of York's Inspiring Digital Enterprise Awards|publisher=University of Huddersfield|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=27 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180527023020/https://www.hud.ac.uk/news/interviews/dukeofyorksinspiringdigitalenterpriseawards/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wigan.gov.uk/Council/Digital-Wigan/iDEA.aspx|title=iDEA|publisher=Wigan Council|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=26 March 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250326134235/https://www.wigan.gov.uk/Council/Digital-Wigan/iDEA.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref> the Duke of York Award for Technical Education, presented to talented young people in technical fields,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.utchub.com/the-duke-of-york-award/|title=The Duke of York Award|publisher=The University Technical College Network|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=26 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180526191140/https://www.utchub.com/the-duke-of-york-award/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.educate.co.uk/duke-york-award-technical-education-educates-involvement/|title=The Duke of York Award for Technical Education: Educate's involvement|publisher=Educate School Services Ltd|date=7 July 2015|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=27 May 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180527022855/https://www.educate.co.uk/duke-york-award-technical-education-educates-involvement/|url-status=dead}}</ref> and the Duke of York Young Entrepreneur Award, which recognised young entrepreneurial talent.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.york.ac.uk/news-and-events/news/2016/business/duke-of-york-entrepreneur/|title=York graduate awarded Duke of York Young Entrepreneur Award|publisher=The University of York|date=27 April 2016|access-date=26 May 2018|archive-date=19 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241219140235/https://www.york.ac.uk/news-and-events/news/2016/business/duke-of-york-entrepreneur/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Andrew was additionally involved with the private limited company the Duke of York's Community Initiative (known as the Yorkshire Foundation between 2005 and 2011) and with a separate charity of a similar name, both of which supported voluntary organisations in Yorkshire. The company and the charity were dissolved in 2023 and 2024, respectively.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/05476536|title=The Duke of York's Community Initiative|work=GOV.UK|access-date=15 October 2025|archive-date=11 September 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250911132849/https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/05476536|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://register-of-charities.charitycommission.gov.uk/en/charity-search/-/charity-details/4030342/charity-overview?_uk_gov_ccew_onereg_charitydetails_web_portlet_CharityDetailsPortlet_organisationNumber=4030342|title=The Duke of York's Community Initiative|work=Charity Commission for England and Wales|access-date=15 October 2025|archive-date=20 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251220125839/https://register-of-charities.charitycommission.gov.uk/en/charity-search/-/charity-details/4030342/charity-overview?_uk_gov_ccew_onereg_charitydetails_web_portlet_CharityDetailsPortlet_organisationNumber=4030342|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Trade and commercial activities==
===Special Representative for International Trade and Investment===
[[File:Putin State Visit to the UK 03.jpg|thumb|upright=0.75|Andrew with Russian President [[Vladimir Putin]] in London, 26 June 2003]]
From 2001 to July 2011, Andrew worked with [[UK Trade & Investment]], part of the [[Department for Business, Innovation and Skills]], as the United Kingdom's Special Representative for International Trade and Investment.<ref>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-14235330 "Prince Andrew to stand down as UK trade envoy"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250503215751/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-14235330 |date=3 May 2025 }}, BBC News, 21 July 2011</ref> The post, previously held by [[Prince Edward, Duke of Kent]], involved representing and promoting the UK at trade fairs and conferences around the world.<ref name="BBC-2011"/> His suitability for the role was challenged in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] by Shadow Justice Minister [[Chris Bryant]] in February 2011, during the [[2011 Libyan civil war]], on the grounds that he was "not only a very close friend of [[Saif al-Islam Gaddafi]], but also&nbsp;... a close friend of the convicted Libyan gun smuggler Tarek Kaituni".<ref>[https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-12604190 "Duke of York must lose trade job, says Labour MP"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407210327/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-12604190 |date=7 April 2025 }}, BBC News, 1 March 2011.</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Libyan gun smuggler was at Princess Eugenie's royal wedding |url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/society/article/libyan-gun-smuggler-at-royal-wedding-tbtmgsnxb |work=[[The Times]] |date=15 October 2018 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=27 May 2025 |access-date=2 April 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527191330/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/society/article/libyan-gun-smuggler-at-royal-wedding-tbtmgsnxb |url-status=live }}</ref>

Further criticism arose after Andrew hosted a lunch for [[Sakher El Materi]], a member of the corrupt Tunisian regime, at Buckingham Palace around the time of the [[Tunisian Revolution]].<ref name="BBC-2011"/> Andrew also formed a friendship with [[Ilham Aliyev]], the president of Azerbaijan, who has been criticised for corruption and human-rights abuses by [[Amnesty International]], and visited him both during and after his tenure as the trade envoy. As of November 2014, Andrew had met Aliyev on 12 occasions.<ref>{{cite news |last=Armitage |first=Jim |date=12 November 2014 |title=Duke of York to meet Azeri despot Ilham Aliyev for 12th time |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/business/news/duke-of-york-to-meet-azeri-despot-ilham-aliyev-for-12th-time-9857222.html |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |access-date=29 November 2014 |archive-date=22 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241222130112/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/business/news/duke-of-york-to-meet-azeri-despot-ilham-aliyev-for-12th-time-9857222.html |url-status=live }}</ref> These controversies, together with his ties to Epstein, led him to step down from the role in 2011.<ref name="BBC-2011"/>

[[File:Ilham Aliyev met with Duke of York of the Great Britain, Prince Andrew.jpg|thumb|With Azerbaijani president [[Ilham Aliyev]], 2011]]
Reports indicated that Andrew maintained close ties with the [[Saudi royal family]], which was deemed helpful to British trade interests, particularly in the defense sector.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davis |first1=Rowenna |title=Prince Andrew pulls out of Saudi Arabia trade trip |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2011/mar/12/prince-andrew-saudi-arabia |work=The Guardian |date=12 March 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Miller |first1=Phil |title=Prince Andrew helped deepen UK relations with Gulf regimes for eight years after Epstein scandal |url=https://www.declassifieduk.org/prince-andrew-helped-deepen-uk-relations-with-gulf-regimes-for-eight-years-after-epstein-scandal/ |work=[[Declassified UK]] |date=25 February 2021}}</ref> Andrew did not receive a salary from the UK Trade & Investment for his role as Special Representative, but he travelled on expenses-paid delegations and was alleged to have occasionally used government-funded trips for personal leisure, earning him the nickname "Airmiles Andy" in the press.<ref name="BBC-2011" /> On 8 March 2011, ''[[The Daily Telegraph]]'' reported: "In 2010, the Prince spent £620,000 as a trade envoy, including £154,000 on hotels, food and hospitality and £465,000 on travel."<ref>{{cite news|first=Steven|last=Swinford|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/8364871/Duke-of-York-costs-taxpayers-15m.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/8364871/Duke-of-York-costs-taxpayers-15m.html |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Duke of York costs taxpayers £15m|newspaper=The Daily Telegraph|location=London|date=7 March 2011|access-date=8 March 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref> In 2026, an anonymous former civil servant alleged that while serving as trade envoy, Andrew used taxpayer money for "massage services".<ref>{{cite news|author1=Sean Coughlan |title=Andrew charged taxpayers for massage when envoy, claim ex-civil servants |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cdr232j8kgvo |website=[[BBC News]] |access-date=23 February 2026 |date=23 February 2026}}</ref>

Official documents relating to Andrew's business trips between 2001 and 2011 will not be released by the [[Foreign Office]] until 2065.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-andrew-files-royal-family-b2404639.html|title=Prince Andrew files to remain secret until 2065|work=The Independent|first=Alexander|last=Butler|date=4 September 2023|accessdate=5 September 2023|archive-date=23 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241223104759/https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-andrew-files-royal-family-b2404639.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In May 2026, the government released papers concerning Andrew's trade role, including a February 2000 [[memorandum]] by [[David Wright (British diplomat)|Sir David Wright]], then chief executive of British Trade International. The memo stated that Queen Elizabeth II had been "very keen" for Andrew to take on a "prominent role in the promotion of national interests" and that it was her "wish" that he take over the role from the Duke of Kent as the UK's special representative for trade.<ref name="AndrewTradeRole2026">{{cite news |last=Devlin |first=Kate |date=21 May 2026 |title=Queen Elizabeth pushed for Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor to get trade role – and he wasn't vetted, bombshell files show |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/prince-andrew-mountbatten-windsor-trade-uk-golf-epstein-b2981044.html |access-date=21 May 2026 |work=The Independent}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Amos |first=Owen |date=21 May 2026 |title=Andrew preferred to visit 'more sophisticated countries', trade envoy files show |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/live/c707eng8rzet |access-date=21 May 2026 |work=BBC News}}</ref>

====Alleged comments on corruption and Kazakhstan====
As the United Kingdom's Special Trade Representative, Andrew travelled widely to promote British businesses. The [[United States diplomatic cables leak]] revealed that [[Tatiana Gfoeller]], the [[United States Ambassador to Kyrgyzstan]], had reported Andrew discussing bribery in Kyrgyzstan and the investigation into the [[Al-Yamamah arms deal]]. According to Gfoeller, he was referring to an investigation, later closed, into alleged kickbacks a senior Saudi royal was said to have received in connection with a multi‑year [[BAE Systems]] contract.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/international/archive/2010/11/cablegate-chronicles-prince-andrew-on-these-expletive-journalists/67383/|title=Cablegate Chronicles: Prince Andrew on 'These (Expletive) Journalists'|magazine=[[The Atlantic]]|date=29 November 2010|access-date=11 August 2019|archive-date=25 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241225062405/https://www.theatlantic.com/international/archive/2010/11/cablegate-chronicles-prince-andrew-on-these-expletive-journalists/67383/|url-status=live}}</ref> The dispatch reported that those present, described as his mother's subjects, reacted with approval. It stated that he criticised journalists, particularly those from ''[[The Guardian]]'', saying they "poke their noses everywhere", and that he claimed this made business more difficult for British companies. It added that the group "practically clapped".<ref name="Leigh-2010">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2010/nov/29/wikileaks-cables-rude-prince-andrew|title=WikiLeaks cables: 'Rude' Prince Andrew shocks US ambassador|newspaper=The Guardian|first1=David|last1=Leigh|first2=Heather|last2=Brooke|first3=Rob|last3=Evans|date=29 November 2010|access-date=11 August 2019|archive-date=29 November 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101129230413/http://www.guardian.co.uk/uk/2010/nov/29/wikileaks-cables-rude-prince-andrew|url-status=live}}</ref>

In May 2008, Andrew attended a goose-hunt in [[Kazakhstan]] with President [[Nursultan Nazarbayev]].<ref>{{Cite news|last1=Foggo|first1=Daniel|first2=Steven| last2=Swinford|first3=Anna|last3=Mikhailova|title=Prince Andrew, his £15m home and the Kazakhstan connection|newspaper=[[The Times]]| date=27 July 2008|location=London|url=http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/uk/article4407240.ece|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080904234122/http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/uk/article4407240.ece|archive-date=4 September 2008|quote=On one of his most recent visits, in May, he [Prince Andrew] is understood to have spent a weekend on a goose-shooting excursion with Nursultan Nazarbayev, the president of Kazakhstan. ... Timur Kulibayev, 41, is a billionaire oil and gas tycoon who is known to Andrew, not least through their attendance at hunting parties thrown by Nazarbayev. ... Kulibayev has also been busy inviting VIPs, reportedly including the prince, to regular hunting meets hosted by the Kazakh president.}}</ref> In 2010, it emerged that the president's son-in-law, [[Timur Kulibayev]], had paid Andrew's representatives £15 million{{snd}}£3 million above the asking price{{snd}}via offshore companies, for Andrew's [[Surrey]] mansion, Sunninghill Park.<ref name="Leigh-2010" /> A BBC investigation later reported that Kulibayev had financed the purchase in part through a loan from Enviro Pacific Investments, a firm which Italian prosecutors concluded had received funds linked to a 2007 bribery scheme.<ref name="BBC-Kulibayev">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8d09qd6zn2o|title=Oligarch linked to bribery paid Andrew £15m for mansion|work=BBC News|first=James|last=Oliver|first2=Will|last2=Dahlgreen|first3=Andy|last3=Verity|date=8 January 2026|access-date=8 January 2026|archive-date=8 January 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260108064937/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c8d09qd6zn2o|url-status=live}}</ref> The BBC further reported that the final payments associated with the alleged scheme occurred only weeks before contracts were exchanged for Sunninghill Park, raising questions about whether Andrew may have inadvertently benefited from the proceeds of crime, and whether appropriate due-diligence checks had been carried out.<ref name="BBC-Kulibayev"/> Kulibayev denied any involvement in bribery or corruption, has not been charged, and his lawyers stated that the loan had been obtained on commercial terms and later repaid with interest.<ref name="BBC-Kulibayev"/> He frequently appeared in US [[diplomatic cables]] as one of the individuals who had accumulated significant wealth in gas-rich Kazakhstan.<ref name="Leigh-2010" /> It was later reported that Andrew's office had attempted to secure a crown estate property close to [[Kensington Palace]] for Kulibayev at the time.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2016/jul/03/prince-andrew-broker-crown-property-kazakh-oligarch|title=Prince Andrew tried to broker crown property deal for Kazakh oligarch|work=The Guardian|first=Robert|last=Booth|date=3 July 2016|accessdate=1 July 2022}}</ref>
[[File:นายกรัฐมนตรี เป็นเจ้าภาพถวายเลี้ยงพระกระยาหารกลางวันแด - Flickr - Abhisit Vejjajiva (4).jpg|thumb|Andrew with Thailand's Prime Minister [[Abhisit Vejjajiva]] in October 2009]]
In May 2012, Swiss and Italian police investigating "a network of personal and business relationships" allegedly used for "international corruption" examined the activities of Enviro Pacific Investments, which charged "multi-million pound fees" to energy companies seeking to operate in Kazakhstan.<ref name="Lewis-2012">{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/9291896/Money-laundering-probe-puts-spotlight-on-the-15-million-sale-of-the-Duke-of-Yorks-home.html |title=Money laundering probe puts spotlight on the £15 million sale of the Duke of York's home |author=Lewis, Jason |work=The Sunday Telegraph|location=London |date=26 May 2012 |access-date=26 May 2012 |url-status=dead |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120526222822/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/9291896/Money-laundering-probe-puts-spotlight-on-the-15-million-sale-of-the-Duke-of-Yorks-home.html |archive-date=26 May 2012 }}</ref> The trust is believed to have paid £6 million towards the purchase of Sunninghill, which now appears derelict.<ref name="Lewis-2012" /> A Palace spokesman responded: "This was a private sale between two trusts. There was never any impropriety on the part of The Duke of York".<ref name="Lewis-2012" /> [[Libby Purves]] wrote in ''[[The Times]]'' in January 2015: "Prince Andrew dazzles easily when confronted with immense wealth and apparent power. He has fallen for 'friendships' with bad, corrupt and clever men, not only in the US but in [[Libya]], Kazakhstan, [[Uzbekistan]], [[Tunisia]], wherever."<ref name="Greenslade-2015">Greenslade, Roy [https://www.theguardian.com/media/greenslade/2015/jan/05/prince-andrew-story-runs-and-runs-but-editors-should-beware "Prince Andrew story runs and runs – but editors should beware"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170316025803/https://www.theguardian.com/media/greenslade/2015/jan/05/prince-andrew-story-runs-and-runs-but-editors-should-beware |date=16 March 2017 }}, ''The Guardian'' (blog), 5 January 2015</ref>

In May 2016, a further controversy arose when the ''Daily Mail'' alleged that Andrew had brokered a deal to assist a Greek and Swiss consortium in securing a £385-million contract to build water and sewerage networks in two of Kazakhstan's largest cities while serving as trade envoy, and that he stood to gain a £4-million commission. The newspaper published an email from Andrew to Kazakh oligarch Kenges Rakishev{{snd}}who had allegedly brokered the sale of Sunninghill Park{{snd}}and reported that Rakishev had arranged meetings for the consortium. After initially stating that the email was a forgery, Buckingham Palace sought to block its publication as a privacy breach.<ref name="Sawer-2016">{{cite news|last=Sawer|first=Patrick |title=Prince Andrew brokered £385m deal with Kazakh regime while working as British trade envoy|work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|date= 21 May 2016|url= https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2016/05/20/duke-brokered-385m-for-corrupt-regime-while-working-as-british-t/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2016/05/20/duke-brokered-385m-for-corrupt-regime-while-working-as-british-t/ |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live}}{{cbignore}}</ref> The Palace denied that Andrew had acted as a "fixer" calling the article "untrue, defamatory and a breach of the editor's code of conduct".<ref name="Sawer-2016" /> Former Foreign Office minister, MP [[Chris Bryant]] commented: "When I was at the Foreign Office it was very difficult to see in whose interests he [Andrew] was acting. He doesn't exactly add lustre to the Royal diadem".<ref name="Sawer-2016" />

====Arms sales====
During his tenure as the UK's Special Representative for International Trade and Investment, Andrew faced significant controversy regarding his role in fostering [[UK arms export|arms deals with Saudi Arabia]], particularly in relation to alleged bribery and corruption involving BAE Systems.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Evans |first1=Rob |last2=Leigh |first2=David |title=WikiLeaks cables: Prince Andrew demanded special BAE briefing |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2010/nov/30/prince-andrew-wikileaks-cables |work=The Guardian |date=30 November 2010}}</ref> In March 2011, Kaye Stearman of the [[Campaign Against the Arms Trade]] (CAAT) told [[Channel 4 News]] that the organisation viewed Andrew as part of a wider problem. She argued that he helped advance arms sales on behalf of [[UKTI]].<ref>[http://www.channel4.com/news/prince-andrew-cheerleader-in-chief-for-the-arms-industry "Prince Andrew: 'Cheerleader in chief for the arms industry'"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250329184742/https://www.channel4.com/news/prince-andrew-cheerleader-in-chief-for-the-arms-industry |date=29 March 2025 }}. Channel 4 News. 10 March 2011.</ref>

In January 2014, Andrew took part in a delegation to [[Bahrain]], a close ally of the United Kingdom. Andrew Smith, a spokesman for CAAT, said: "We are calling on Prince Andrew and the UK government to stop selling arms to Bahrain. By endorsing the [[Human rights in Bahrain|Bahraini dictatorship]] Prince Andrew is giving his implicit support to their oppressive practices. When our government sells arms it is giving moral and practical support to an illegitimate and authoritarian regime and directly supporting their systematic crackdown on opposition groups. (...) We shouldn't allow our international image to be used as a PR tool for the violent and oppressive dictatorship in Bahrain."<ref>[https://www.caat.org.uk/media/press-releases/2014-01-15 "Campaigners call for UK to halt arms exports to Bahrain as Prince Andrew joins sales drive"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150119003303/https://www.caat.org.uk/media/press-releases/2014-01-15 |date=19 January 2015 }}. Campaign Against Arms Trade. 15 January 2014.</ref> Smith also stated: "The prince has consistently used his position to promote arms sales and boost some of the most unpleasant governments in the world, his arms sales haven't just given military support to corrupt and repressive regimes. They've lent those regimes political and international legitimacy."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://america.aljazeera.com/articles/2015/1/17/prince-andrew-britainelites.html|title=What Scandal Involving Prince Andrew Says – Al Jazeera America|access-date=19 April 2016|archive-date=22 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241222181005/http://america.aljazeera.com/articles/2015/1/17/prince-andrew-britainelites.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Promotion of Banque Havilland===
In November 2020, following reviews of emails, internal documents, and unreported regulatory filings, as well as interviews with former bank insiders, ''[[Bloomberg Businessweek]]'' reported that Andrew had used his royal status and his role as trade envoy to assist [[David Rowland (property developer)|David Rowland]] and his private bank, [[Banque Havilland]], in securing clients around the world.<ref name="bloomberg-2020"/> The Rowland family were among Andrew's investment advisers,<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.ft.com/content/b97cea16-ad05-11e7-beba-5521c713abf4 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20221210/https://www.ft.com/content/b97cea16-ad05-11e7-beba-5521c713abf4 |archive-date=10 December 2022 | title=Former Conservative party treasurer wins Luxembourg bank case | date=9 October 2017 | accessdate=30 April 2019 | last=Croft | first=Jane | newspaper=[[Financial Times]] | url-access=subscription}}</ref> and he attended the bank's official opening ceremony in July 2009.<ref>{{cite web|title=Ouverture officielle Banque Havilland en présence de S. A. R le Duc d'York K. G.|trans-title=Official Opening of Banque Havilland in the presence of HRH the Duke of York, KG|url=https://paperjam.lu/article/communique-ouverture-officielle-banque-havilland-en-presence-de-sar-le-duc-dyork-kg|website=Paperjam.lu|accessdate=15 February 2017|archive-date=23 August 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250823213453/https://paperjam.lu/article/communique-ouverture-officielle-banque-havilland-en-presence-de-sar-le-duc-dyork-kg|url-status=live}}</ref> In his email exchanges with [[Jeffrey Epstein]] in May 2010, Andrew described Rowland as his "trusted money man" although despite Andrew's encouragement for Epstein to invest in the Rowlands' venture he appeared to be reluctant.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1kldw1e0w3o|title=Epstein emails: So-called 'shady financier' was Andrew's 'trusted money man'|work=BBC News|first=Ben|last=King|first2=Sean|last2=Coughlan|date=6 February 2025|access-date=7 February 2025|archive-date=6 February 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260206163806/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1kldw1e0w3o|url-status=live}}</ref> It has been alleged that Banque Havilland sought to service dictators and kleptocrats.<ref name="bloomberg-2020">{{cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/features/2020-11-19/prince-andrew-helped-david-rowland-and-banque-havilland-woo-sketchy-clients|title=Prince Andrew Helped a Secretive Luxembourg Bank Woo Sketchy Clients|work=Bloomberg Businessweek|first1=Gavin|last1=Finch|first2=Harry|last2=Wilson|date=19 November 2020|accessdate=26 October 2021|archive-date=9 September 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250909124530/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/features/2020-11-19/prince-andrew-helped-david-rowland-and-banque-havilland-woo-sketchy-clients|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2021, [[Bloomberg News]] reported that a firm connected to Rowland had been paying off Andrew's debts.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2021/nov/16/prince-andrews-15m-loan-paid-off-by-firms-linked-to-tory-donor-report|title=Prince Andrew's £1.5m loan paid off by firms linked to Tory donor – report|date=16 November 2021|accessdate=24 November 2021|website=The Guardian|first=Heather|last=Stewart}}</ref> In November 2017, Andrew borrowed £250,000 from Banque Havilland, adding to an existing £1.25&nbsp;million loan that had been "extended or increased 10 times" since 2015.<ref name="Wilson-2021">{{cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-11-16/prince-andrew-s-2-million-loan-paid-off-by-top-political-donor|title=Prince Andrew's £1.5 Million Loan Paid Off by Top Political Donor|work=Bloomberg Businessweek|first1=Harry|last1=Wilson|first2=Gavin|last2=Finch|date=16 November 2021|accessdate=3 February 2022|archive-date=2 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250602231937/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-11-16/prince-andrew-s-2-million-loan-paid-off-by-top-political-donor|url-status=live}}</ref> Documents indicated that although the "credibility of the applicant" had been questioned, the loan was approved in an effort to "further business potential with the Royal Family".<ref name="Wilson-2021"/> Eleven days later, in December 2017, £1.5&nbsp;million was transferred from an account at Albany Reserves{{snd}}controlled by the Rowland family{{snd}}to Andrew's account at Banque Havilland, paying off the loan due in March 2018.<ref name="Wilson-2021"/>

In February 2026, ''The Daily Telegraph'' reported that, in February 2010, while serving as the UK's trade envoy, Andrew forwarded a confidential Treasury briefing on the [[Icelandic financial crisis]] to [[Jonathan Rowland]], the chief executive of Banque Havilland.<ref>{{cite news|title=Andrew leaked confidential Treasury briefing to banker friend |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2026/02/13/andrew-leaked-confidential-treasury-briefing-banker-iceland/?WT.mc_id=tmgoff_tw_post_confidential-treasury-briefing-banker-iceland/ |website=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=13 February 2026 |date=13 February 2026}} {{subscription required}}</ref>

===Relationship with alleged Chinese spy===
{{Further|Chinese espionage in the United Kingdom}}
In December 2024, it was reported that Andrew had invited [[Chris Yang]], a Chinese businessman initially identified as "H6" in legal documents, to events at royal residences. Yang had been authorised by a royal aide, Dominic Hampshire, to act on Andrew's behalf when dealing with potential investors in China. Yang was barred from entering the United Kingdom in 2023 due to alleged involvement in "covert and deceptive activity" on behalf of the [[Chinese Communist Party]]. Andrew ceased all contact with Yang following government concerns.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/dec/13/prince-andrew-confidant-loses-appeal-on-uk-ban-over-national-security|title=Prince Andrew 'confidant' loses appeal on UK ban over national security|date=13 December 2024|work=[[The Guardian]]|accessdate=14 December 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/prince-andrew-invited-chinese-spy-buckingham-palace-qqpgsvvg6|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260219185043/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/prince-andrew-invited-chinese-spy-buckingham-palace-qqpgsvvg6|title=Prince Andrew invited 'Chinese spy' into Buckingham Palace|date=13 December 2024|work=[[The Times]]|accessdate=14 December 2024|archivedate=2026-02-19}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|date=16 December 2024 |title=Sir Keir Starmer defends China policy after spy scandal |url=https://uk.news.yahoo.com/identifying-chinese-spy-alleged-links-073500309.html |access-date=16 December 2024 |website=Yahoo News |language=en-GB |archive-date=28 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250828034046/https://uk.news.yahoo.com/identifying-chinese-spy-alleged-links-073500309.html |url-status=live }}</ref> According to a 2025 report by ''[[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]]'', UK intelligence agencies deemed Andrew a potential national‑security risk because of his repeated meetings and close relationship with Yang, with concerns dating back to 2021 that his vulnerability and access could be exploited.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2025/10/20/prince-andrew-deemed-threat-by-security-services/|title=Prince Andrew deemed a threat by security services|work=The Telegraph|first1=Gordon|last1=Rayner|first2=Ben|last2=Riley-Smith|date=20 October 2025|access-date=21 October 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251021054240/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2025/10/20/prince-andrew-deemed-threat-by-security-services/|archive-date=21 October 2025|url-status=live|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

In 2025, it was reported that Andrew had met [[Cai Qi]]{{snd}}who later became the [[20th Secretariat of the Chinese Communist Party|first-ranked member]] of the [[Secretariat of the Chinese Communist Party]] (CCP) and ''de facto'' [[chief of staff]] to [[Xi Jinping]]{{snd}}in London in 2018 and in Beijing in 2018 and 2019.<ref name="Gooch-2025">{{cite news|url=https://www.the-independent.com/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-cai-qi-china-spies-meeting-b2847222.html|title=Prince Andrew 'met China spymaster at least three times'|work=The Independent|first=Bryony|last=Gooch|date=17 October 2025|access-date=21 October 2025}}</ref> Cai had been suspected of receiving sensitive information from British nationals Christopher Cash and Christopher Berry, though charges against them were dropped by the [[Crown Prosecution Service]] in 2025.<ref name="Gooch-2025" /> The London meeting formed part of a welcome event for a Beijing delegation attended by then-Labour leader [[Jeremy Corbyn]], Scottish former first minister [[Nicola Sturgeon]], former Cabinet Office minister [[David Lidington]], and London Mayor [[Sadiq Khan]].<ref name="Gooch-2025"/> Andrew's subsequent meetings with Cai were connected to the expansion and launch of his Pitch@Palace business initiative in China.<ref name="Gooch-2025"/>

==Finances==
Andrew received a £249,000 annuity from Queen Elizabeth II, which was reduced by King Charles III in April 2023.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.royal.gov.uk/TheRoyalHousehold/Royalfinances/FinancialarrangmentsofothermembersoftheRoyalFamily.aspx |publisher=Royal Household |title=Financial arrangements of members of the Royal Family |access-date=7 March 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110122004226/http://www.royal.gov.uk/TheRoyalHousehold/Royalfinances/FinancialarrangmentsofothermembersoftheRoyalFamily.aspx |archive-date=22 January 2011 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2023/02/19/king-charles-has-vowed-not-leave-prince-andrew-homeless-penniless/|title=King Charles 'has vowed not to leave Prince Andrew homeless or penniless'|work=The Telegraph|first=Ewan|last=Somerville|date=19 February 2023|accessdate=22 February 2023|url-access=subscription|archive-date=25 January 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250125171800/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2023/02/19/king-charles-has-vowed-not-leave-prince-andrew-homeless-penniless/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the twelve-month period up to April 2004, he spent £325,000 on flights, and his trade missions as special representative for [[UKTI]] cost £75,000 in 2003.<ref name="Barkham-2004"/> ''[[The Sunday Times]]'' reported in July 2008 that, for "the Duke of York's public role{{nbsp}}... he last year received £436,000 to cover his expenses".<ref>{{cite news|last1=Foggo|first1=Daniel|last2=Swinford|first2=Steven|last3=Mikhailova|first3=Anna|url=https://www.thetimes.com/article/prince-andrew-his-pound15m-home-and-the-kazakhstan-connection-l6ldqp0fq7l|title=Prince Andrew, his £15m home and the Kazakhstan connection|work=[[The Sunday Times]]|location=London|date=27 July 2008|access-date=24 November 2019|url-access=subscription}}</ref> He also receives a Royal Navy pension of £20,000.<ref name="Wilson-2021"/>

In June 2019, Andrew arranged a private Buckingham Palace tour for [[Jay Bloom]] and Michael Evers, businessmen from the US cryptocurrency mining company Pegasus Group Holdings, which had agreed to pay his ex-wife up to £1.4 million for her role as a "brand ambassador".<ref name="BBC-crypto">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy5q05v0q1xo|title=Andrew fixed palace visit for firm with £1.4m deal with ex-wife|work=[[BBC News Online|BBC News]]|first1=Billy|last1=Kenber|first2=Phil|last2=Kemp|date=1 November 2025|access-date=3 November 2025|archive-date=3 November 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251103043049/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy5q05v0q1xo|url-status=live}}</ref> Bloom and Evers were driven into the Palace in Andrew's car from their Knightsbridge hotel and later attended his Pitch@Palace event at St James's Palace before dining that evening with Andrew, Ferguson, and their daughter Beatrice.<ref name="BBC-crypto"/> Ferguson was promoting Pegasus's plan to use thousands of solar-powered generators to mine Bitcoin in Arizona, though the project collapsed after acquiring only 615 of the planned 16,000 units and generating just $33,779 (£25,000) in cryptocurrency.<ref name="BBC-crypto"/> Ferguson first met Bloom in Las Vegas in 2018, and he and Evers visited London frequently in 2019, meeting the York family on several occasions.<ref name="BBC-crypto"/> In October 2019, Ferguson signed a contract via Alphabet Capital, a British company owned by Adrian Gleave, through which she was paid more than £200,000 for Pegasus-related work.<ref name="BBC-crypto"/> Court documents showed that Andrew also received £60,500 traced to Gleave's businesses, though neither party explained the payments.<ref name="BBC-crypto"/>

Several months after Andrew's controversial 2019 ''[[Newsnight]]'' interview, his private office established the Urramoor Trust, which owned both Lincelles (established 2020)<ref>{{Cite web|title=Lincelles|url=https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12699827|access-date=7 January 2022|website=Companies House|archive-date=18 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241218123303/https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/12699827|url-status=live}}</ref> and Urramoor Ltd (established 2013),<ref>{{Cite web|title=Urramoor Limited|url=https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/08424933|access-date=7 January 2022|website=Companies House|archive-date=26 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426175442/https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/08424933|url-status=live}}</ref> and was, according to ''[[The Times]],'' set up to support his family. Lincelles was voluntarily wound up in 2022.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/law/article/prince-andrew-winds-up-investment-company-linked-to-disgraced-coutts-banker-harry-keogh-f6tn3vgpt|title=Prince Andrew winds up investment company linked to disgraced Coutts banker Harry Keogh|work=[[The Times]]|first=George|last=Greenwood|date=5 January 2022|access-date=6 January 2022|url-access=subscription|archive-date=27 May 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527191333/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/law/article/prince-andrew-winds-up-investment-company-linked-to-disgraced-coutts-banker-harry-keogh-f6tn3vgpt|url-status=live}}</ref> Andrew was described as a "[[settlor]] but not a beneficiary" and did not own either company, though [[Companies House]] listed him and his long-time private banker Harry Keogh as persons with "significant control".<ref name="Royston-2021">{{cite news|url=https://www.newsweek.com/prince-andrew-coutts-banker-harry-keogh-accused-sexual-harassment-new-business-lincelles-1586631|title=Prince Andrew Teams Up With Banker Accused of Sexual Harassment For New Business Venture|work=Newsweek|first=Jack|last=Royston|date=27 April 2021|accessdate=6 January 2022|archive-date=26 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426194101/https://www.newsweek.com/prince-andrew-coutts-banker-harry-keogh-accused-sexual-harassment-new-business-lincelles-1586631|url-status=live}}</ref>

In March 2022, it was reported that, on 15 November 2019, the wife of jailed former Turkish politician [[İlhan İşbilen]] had transferred £750,000 to Andrew in the belief that it would help her secure a passport.<ref name="Dixon-2022">{{cite news|title=Prince Andrew took £1 million from Turkish 'fraudster' |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/03/31/duke-york-took-1m-turkish-fraudster/ |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |first1=Hayley |last1=Dixon |first2=Robert |last2=Mendick |first3=Victoria |last3=Ward |date=31 March 2022 |access-date=1 April 2022 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=8 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251008015807/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/03/31/duke-york-took-1m-turkish-fraudster/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He repaid the money 16 months later after being contacted by İşbilen's lawyers. ''The Telegraph'' reported that the payment had been described to the bankers "as a wedding gift" for his elder daughter, Beatrice, though court documents did not suggest that Beatrice was aware of the transaction.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/04/01/princess-beatrice-eugenie-prince-andrew-fraud-money-news/|title=Exclusive: Princesses Beatrice and Eugenie received money from Turkish 'fraudster' linked to Prince Andrew|work=The Telegraph|first1=Hayley|last1=Dixon|first2=Victoria|last2=Ward|first3=Robert|last3=Mendick|date=1 April 2022|accessdate=3 April 2022|url-access=subscription|archive-date=13 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250613013537/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/04/01/princess-beatrice-eugenie-prince-andrew-fraud-money-news/|url-status=live}}</ref> İşbilen alleges that a further £350,000 was paid to Andrew through businessman Selman Turk, who she is suing for fraud. Turk had received the People's Choice Award for his business Heyman AI at a Pitch@Palace event held at St James's Palace days before the £750,000 transfer.<ref name="Dixon-2022"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/apr/04/new-questions-raised-over-prince-andrews-award-to-selman-turk|title=New questions raised over Prince Andrew's award to Selman Turk|work=[[The Guardian]]|first=Jamie|last=Grierson|date=4 April 2022|accessdate=5 April 2022}}</ref> In October 2025, it was reported that in December 2019 Andrew received £60,500 from Adrian Gleave, whose company Alphabet Capital Limited had also funneled money from Nebahat İşbilen to Andrew and Ferguson.<ref name="Kenber-2025">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cy5qrp2wne4o|title=Prince Andrew paid by businessman tied to pension rip-off company|work=BBC News|first1=Billy|last1=Kenber|first2=Phil|last2=Kemp|date=24 October 2025|access-date=24 October 2025}}</ref> Court documents indicated that Alphabet Capital had made{{snd}}and might continue to make{{snd}}substantial payments to Andrew, despite being listed as a dormant company with minimal turnover.<ref name="Kenber-2025"/> Gleave had links to SVS Securities, a firm shut down by regulators over pension mis-selling.<ref name="Kenber-2025"/>

Tarek Kaituni, a Libyan-born convicted gun smuggler, introduced Andrew to Selman Turk in May or June 2019 and met him on at least two occasions.<ref name="Grierson-2022">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/apr/03/more-details-emerge-of-prince-andrews-alleged-links-with-banker-selman-turk|title=More details emerge of Prince Andrew's alleged links with banker Selman Turk|work=[[The Guardian]]|first=Jamie|last=Grierson|date=3 April 2022|accessdate=3 April 2022}}</ref> Kaituni, for whom Andrew had allegedly lobbied a British company, had reportedly given Beatrice an £18,000 gold and diamond necklace for her 21st birthday in 2009 and was invited to Eugenie's wedding in 2018.<ref name="Grierson-2022"/> Andrew also received "half" of £100,000 that Turk claimed was a payment to businessman Adrian Gleave to fund a search for "finding yoghurt production facilities in America".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2023/12/19/prince-andrew-invited-fraudster-smuggler-st-james-palace/|title=Prince Andrew invited alleged fraudster and smuggler to St James's Palace, documents reveal|work=The Telegraph|first=Fiona|last=Parker|date=19 December 2023|accessdate=20 December 2023|url-access=subscription|archive-date=24 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241224225849/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2023/12/19/prince-andrew-invited-fraudster-smuggler-st-james-palace/|url-status=live}}</ref> In October 2025, ''[[The Guardian]]'' reported that Andrew was set to receive a one-off six-figure payment from the King's private funds to help finance his move from Royal Lodge to a smaller property on the Sandringham estate.<ref name="Guardian-payments">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/oct/31/andrew-in-line-for-six-figure-payment-and-annual-stipend-from-king-sources-say|title=Andrew in line for six-figure payout and annual stipend from king, sources say|work=The Guardian|first1=Caroline|last1=Davies|first2=Anna|last2=Isaac|first3=Neha|last3=Gohil|first4=Vikram|last4=Dodd|date=31 October 2025|access-date=1 November 2025}}</ref> He would also receive an annual stipend worth several times his £20,000 naval pension.<ref name="Guardian-payments" />

==Epstein scandal==

=== Jeffrey Epstein and related associations ===
{{main|Relationship of Prince Andrew and Jeffrey Epstein}}

[[File:Jeffrey Epstein mug shot (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright=0.75|[[Jeffrey Epstein]] maintained links with Andrew before and after his 2008 conviction.]]
Andrew was a friend of [[Jeffrey Epstein]], an American financier who pleaded guilty in 2008 to soliciting prostitution from a person under the age of 18.<ref name="Pike-2025">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4g7d39n6vgo|title=Prince Andrew hosted Epstein, Maxwell and Weinstein at Royal Lodge|work=[[BBC News Online|BBC News]]|first=Joe|last=Pike|date=27 October 2025|access-date=28 October 2025|archive-date=19 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251219222240/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c4g7d39n6vgo|url-status=live}}</ref> In December 2010, Andrew was photographed walking with Epstein in [[Central Park]] during a visit to New York City.<ref name="BBC News-2019">{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-49411215|title=Prince Andrew's links to Jeffrey Epstein|date=16 November 2019|work=[[BBC News Online|BBC News]]|access-date=4 February 2020|archive-date=1 May 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501104338/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-49411215|url-status=live}}</ref> In July 2011, Andrew's role as trade envoy was terminated, amid escalating controversy over his associations, particularly with Epstein.<ref name="BBC-2011"/><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/jan/10/jeffrey-epstein-decade-scandal-prince-andrew|title=Jeffrey Epstein: inside the decade of scandal entangling Prince Andrew|last1=Lewis|first1=Paul|last2=Swaine|first2=Jon|date=10 January 2015|newspaper=[[The Guardian]]|location=London, England|access-date=22 January 2016|archive-date=22 May 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160522090456/http://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/jan/10/jeffrey-epstein-decade-scandal-prince-andrew|url-status=live}}</ref>

In 2011, [[Virginia Giuffre]], a prominent accuser of Epstein, told the ''[[Daily Mail]]'' that she had never had sexual contact with the then-Prince Andrew, but she later alleged that Epstein trafficked her to Andrew on several occasions.<ref name=":22">{{Cite news|date=3 December 2019|title=Prince Andrew accuser tells BBC she was forced to have sex with the British royal|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/europe/prince-andrew-accuser-tells-bbc-about-being-forced-to-have-sex-with-the-british-royal/2019/12/02/6c550e32-1511-11ea-80d6-d0ca7007273f_story.html|access-date=16 February 2026|work=The Washington Post|language=en-US|issn=0190-8286|archive-date=20 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221220082113/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/europe/prince-andrew-accuser-tells-bbc-about-being-forced-to-have-sex-with-the-british-royal/2019/12/02/6c550e32-1511-11ea-80d6-d0ca7007273f_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref> That same year, ''The Daily Telegraph'' reported that [[Epstein's private jet]] had once landed at [[RAF Marham]] in [[Norfolk]].<ref>{{cite news|author1=Rayner |title=Epstein angered by Telegraph report exposing flights to RAF bases |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2026/02/04/jeffrey-epstein-telegraph-report-exposing-flights-raf-bases/ |website=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=21 February 2026 |date=4 February 2026|url-access=subscription |first=Gordon|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260205131455/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2026/02/04/jeffrey-epstein-telegraph-report-exposing-flights-raf-bases/|url-status=live|archive-date=5 February 2026}}</ref> It has been alleged that Andrew "pulled strings" to enable Epstein to use the RAF base.<ref>{{cite news|author1=Corfield |title=Andrew pulled strings so Epstein could use RAF bases for private jet |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2026/02/01/andrew-pulled-strings-so-epstein-could-use-raf-bases/ |website=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=21 February 2026 |date=1 February 2026|url-access=subscription |first=Gareth}}</ref>

On 30 December 2014, a court filing in [[Florida]] by the lawyers [[Bradley J. Edwards]] and [[Paul G. Cassell]] alleged that Andrew was among several prominent figures, including lawyer [[Alan Dershowitz]] and "a former prime minister",<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-30664060|title=Prince Andrew sex claims woman 'should not be believed'|date=3 January 2015|work=BBC News|archive-date=19 December 2024|access-date=20 June 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241219053935/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-30664060|url-status=live}}</ref> who had participated in sexual activities with a minor later identified as Virginia Giuffre (then known by her maiden name, Virginia Roberts),<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://time.com/3655417/alan-dershowitz-prince-andrew-virginia-roberts/|title=U.S. Lawyer Sues in Prince Andrew Sex Claims Case|last1=Gibson|first1=Megan|date=6 January 2015|access-date=9 January 2015|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|archive-date=1 May 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501104338/https://time.com/3655417/alan-dershowitz-prince-andrew-virginia-roberts/|url-status=dead}}</ref> who was allegedly trafficked by Epstein.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/us/alan-dershowitz-denies-allegations-of-sex-with-minor.html|title=Alan Dershowitz Denies Suit's Allegations of Sex With a Minor|last1=Williams|first1=Timothy|date=6 January 2015|work=The New York Times|access-date=7 January 2015|url-status=live|url-access=limited|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150107214335/http://www.nytimes.com/2015/01/07/us/alan-dershowitz-denies-allegations-of-sex-with-minor.html|archive-date=7 January 2015}}</ref> In January 2015, there was renewed media and public pressure for Buckingham Palace to explain Andrew's connection with Epstein.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2015/jan/06/palace-prince-andrew-links-jeffrey-epstein|title=Palace digs in over Prince Andrew's links to sex offender Jeffrey Epstein|last=Booth|first=Robert|date=9 January 2015|work=The Guardian|access-date=19 April 2016|location=London, England}}</ref> The Palace stated that "any suggestion of impropriety with underage minors is categorically untrue", and later repeated the denial.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/teenage-sex-slave-virginia-roberts-claims-she-was-paid-10000-by-jeffrey-epstein-to-have-sex-with-prince-andrew-at-london-home-9956338.html|title=Teenage 'sex slave' Virginia Roberts claims she was paid £10,000 by Jeffrey Epstein to have sex with Prince Andrew|author=Withnall, Adam|date=4 January 2015|work=The Independent|location=London|archive-date=25 September 2015|access-date=8 September 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150925151827/http://www.independent.co.uk/news/people/teenage-sex-slave-virginia-roberts-claims-she-was-paid-10000-by-jeffrey-epstein-to-have-sex-with-prince-andrew-at-london-home-9956338.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2015/jan/04/prince-andrew-denies-underage-sex-claims-buckingham-palace|title=Palace takes unusual step to deny Prince Andrew underage sex claims|last1=Booth|first1=Robert|date=4 January 2015|newspaper=The Guardian|access-date=15 January 2015|last2=Lewis|first2=Paul|location=London|archive-date=3 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250403014534/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2015/jan/04/prince-andrew-denies-underage-sex-claims-buckingham-palace|url-status=live}}</ref>

Giuffre stated that she had sex with Andrew on three occasions, including during a trip to London in 2001 when she was 17,<ref name="AP NEWS-2019">{{Cite news|url=https://apnews.com/3825458fbca548cea862f09e79f45534|title=Prince Andrew again denies having sex with Epstein victim|date=15 November 2019|website=AP NEWS|access-date=4 February 2020|archive-date=21 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220921221025/https://apnews.com/3825458fbca548cea862f09e79f45534|url-status=live}}</ref> and later in New York and on [[Little Saint James]] in the [[US Virgin Islands]] during an [[orgy]].<ref name="BBC News-2019" /><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/oct/19/prince-andrew-allegations-explainer|title=Prince Andrew: are more revelations likely to emerge about scandal-hit royal?|work=The Guardian|first=Hannah|last=Al-Othman|date=19 October 2025|access-date=19 October 2025}}</ref> She alleged that Epstein paid her $15,000 after she had sex with Andrew in London.<ref name="AP NEWS-2019"/> Flight logs place Andrew and Giuffre in the locations where she said their meetings occurred.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/11359788/Prince-Andrew-under-renewed-pressure-to-speak-about-sex-abuse-claims-after-flight-logs-emerge.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/theroyalfamily/11359788/Prince-Andrew-under-renewed-pressure-to-speak-about-sex-abuse-claims-after-flight-logs-emerge.html |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Prince Andrew under renewed pressure to speak about 'sex abuse' claims after flight logs emerge|date=21 January 2015|work=The Daily Telegraph|access-date=19 April 2016}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/flight-logs-reportedly-link-prince-andrew-to-alleged-jeffrey-epstein-victim-virginia-roberts|title=Flight Logs Reportedly Link Prince Andrew to Alleged Jeffrey Epstein Victim Virginia Roberts|last=Sykes|first=Tom|date=20 August 2019|work=The Daily Beast|access-date=4 February 2020|archive-date=6 February 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250206210021/https://www.thedailybeast.com/flight-logs-reportedly-link-prince-andrew-to-alleged-jeffrey-epstein-victim-virginia-roberts/|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Photograph of Virginia Giuffre, Ghislaine Maxwell and Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor|Andrew and Giuffre were also photographed together]], with his arm around her waist and [[Ghislaine Maxwell]] in the background<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/incoming/article9956876.ece/alternates/w620/v2-8-Virginia-Roberts-Collect-The-Times-v6.jpg|title=The Independent|access-date=19 April 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150723113937/https://www.independent.co.uk/incoming/article9956876.ece/alternates/w620/v2-8-Virginia-Roberts-Collect-The-Times-v6.jpg|archive-date=23 July 2015}}</ref>. Andrew's supporters have repeatedly claimed the image is fake or edited,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2019/08/28/prince-andrews-supporters-say-photo-epstein-victim-fake/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2019/08/28/prince-andrews-supporters-say-photo-epstein-victim-fake/ |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Prince Andrew's supporters say his 'chubby' fingers prove photo of him with Epstein victim is fake|newspaper=The Telegraph|first=Bill|last=Gardner|date=29 August 2019|access-date=30 August 2019}}{{cbignore}}</ref> but most experts consider the photo to be genuine.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Martha |first1=Ross |title=University forensics expert casts doubt on Prince Andrew's claim 'sex slave' photo was faked |url=https://www.thestar.com/news/world/university-forensics-expert-casts-doubt-on-prince-andrew-s-claim-sex-slave-photo-was-faked/article_867b39fa-cab4-5ce9-926b-241660b07f04.html |website=Toronto Star |access-date=4 May 2026 |language=en |date=26 November 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Elser|first=Daniela|url=https://www.news.com.au/entertainment/celebrity-life/royals/unsolved-mysteries-behind-famous-prince-andrew-photo/news-story/b3f883a7df663db2db5de516928ae2b6|archive-url=|title=Unsolved mystery behind notorious photo|date=15 January 2022|work=[[News.com.au]]|accessdate=19 February 2026|archivedate=}}</ref> In addition, an email sent by "G Maxwell" to Epstein in 2015, released as part of the [[Epstein files]], appears to confirm that a photograph had been taken, stating: "In 2001 I was in London when [redacted] met a number of friends of mine including Prince Andrew. A photograph was taken as I imagine she wanted to show it to friends and family."<ref>{{cite news|title=Email appears to confirm Andrew and Virginia Giuffre photo is real |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvgp2xx7gxjo |website=[[BBC News]] |access-date=4 February 2026 |date=4 February 2026 |archive-date=4 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260204190438/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvgp2xx7gxjo |url-status=live }}</ref>

In August 2019, court documents associated with the defamation case between Giuffre and Maxwell revealed that a second woman, Johanna Sjoberg, alleged that Andrew had placed his hand on her breast while posing for a photo with his ''[[Spitting Image]]'' puppet in Epstein's mansion.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/aug/09/prince-andrew-court-documents-ghislaine-maxwell-jeffrey-epstein|title=Prince Andrew groped young woman's breast at Epstein house, court files allege|last1=Bekiempis|first1=Victoria|last2=Waterson|first2=Jim|date=9 August 2019|work=The Guardian|access-date=10 August 2019|issn=0261-3077}}</ref> In January 2026, another Epstein victim alleged, through her lawyer Bradley J. Edwards, that she had been sent to the UK for a sexual encounter with Andrew at Royal Lodge in 2010, when she was in her twenties.<ref name="BBC-sec26">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clymxxlm3xzo|title=Second Epstein victim claims she was sent to UK for sex with Andrew, lawyer says|work=BBC News|first=Chi Chi|last=Izundu|first2=Olivia|last2=Davies|first3=William|last3=Dahlgreen|first4=Adam|last4=Walker|date=31 January 2026|access-date=1 February 2026|archive-date=1 February 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260201003815/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clymxxlm3xzo|url-status=live}}</ref> Her lawyer also said that she was subsequently shown around Buckingham Palace and served tea.<ref name="BBC-sec26" /> In February 2026, the BBC reported on a 2011 legal letter from the [[Epstein files]]. The letter alleged that in 2006, an unnamed [[exotic dancer]] was hired for $10,000 to perform at Epstein's Florida home for him and Andrew and was then propositioned for a [[threesome]]. She stated she was paid only $2,000.<ref>{{Cite news |date=4 February 2026 |title=Andrew and Epstein asked exotic dancer for 'sex acts', legal letter claims |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckgkjj5e28ko |access-date=4 February 2026 |work=BBC News |first=Jessica |last=Rawnsley |first2=Hannah |last2=Price |archive-date=5 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260205230517/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckgkjj5e28ko |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=4 February 2026 |title=Andrew and Epstein asked dancer for threesome, new legal letter claims |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/epstein-andrew-threesome-court-documents-b2913575.html |access-date=4 February 2026 |work=The Independent |first=Josh |last=Payne |archive-date=6 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260206031212/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/epstein-andrew-threesome-court-documents-b2913575.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== ''Newsnight'' interview ===
{{main|Prince Andrew & the Epstein Scandal}}

In November 2019, the BBC's ''[[Newsnight]]'' broadcast an interview between Andrew and presenter [[Emily Maitlis]], in which he discussed his friendship with Epstein publicly for the first time.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/live/uk-50447028?page=4|title=As it happened: Prince Andrew's Interview|website=BBC News|date=16 November 2019|access-date=16 November 2019|archive-date=7 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407165211/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/live/uk-50447028?page=4|url-status=live}}</ref> Andrew said he met Epstein in 1999 through Maxwell, contradicting comments made by his private secretary in 2011 that the two met in "the early 1990s".<ref>{{cite news |title=Letter casts doubt on when prince met Epstein |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-50487104 |access-date=20 November 2019 |work=BBC News |date=20 November 2019 |archive-date=1 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250501104349/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-50487104 |url-status=live }}</ref> Maxwell also disputed Andrew's claims, stating that Ferguson was actually the one that introduced him to Epstein.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Blanche |first1=Todd |title=United States Department of Justice INTERVIEW OF: GHISLAINE MAXWELL |url=https://www.justice.gov/storage/audio-files/Interview%20Transcript/Interview%20Transcript%20-%20Maxwell%202025.07.24%20(Redacted).pdf |website=Department of Justice |access-date=13 April 2026 |page=132-135 |date=24 July 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Debussman |first1=Bernd |title=Ghislaine Maxwell denies seeing 'inappropriate' conduct by Trump |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cdd3pe6189go |website=BBC |access-date=25 April 2026 |date=24 August 2025}}</ref> In the interview, Andrew denied having sex with Giuffre on 10 March 2001, as she alleged, saying he had been at home with his daughters after attending a party at a [[PizzaExpress]] branch in [[Woking]] with his elder daughter, Beatrice.<ref>{{cite news|title=Prince Andrew denies sex with 17-year-old because he was 'at Pizza Express' on night in question|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-interview-pizza-express-virginia-roberts-guiffre-jeffrey-epstein-newsnight-a9205916.html|website=[[The Independent]]|date=16 November 2019|access-date=17 November 2019|archive-date=17 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191117014529/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-interview-pizza-express-virginia-roberts-guiffre-jeffrey-epstein-newsnight-a9205916.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Prince Andrew denies sex with 17-year-old: 'I went to Pizza Express that day'|url=https://news.sky.com/story/prince-andrew-denies-sex-with-17-year-old-i-went-to-pizza-express-that-day-11862902|website=[[Sky News]]|date=16 November 2019|access-date=17 November 2019|archive-date=17 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191117013908/https://news.sky.com/story/prince-andrew-denies-sex-with-17-year-old-i-went-to-pizza-express-that-day-11862902|url-status=live}}</ref>

The interview was believed by Maitlis and the ''Newsnight'' team to have been approved by the Queen,<ref>{{cite news |title=Queen approved Andrew's 'disastrous' interview, Emily Maitlis says |url=https://www.itv.com/news/2019-11-18/queen-approved-andrews-disastrous-interview-emily-maitlis-says/ |access-date=20 November 2019 |work=ITV News |archive-date=9 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250109155440/https://www.itv.com/news/2019-11-18/queen-approved-andrews-disastrous-interview-emily-maitlis-says |url-status=live }}</ref> though "palace insiders" quoted by ''[[The Sunday Telegraph]]'' disputed this.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tominey |first1=Camilla |last2=Ward |first2=Victoria |title=Queen did not approve Prince Andrew's excruciating Newsnight interview |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2019/11/17/queen-did-not-approve-prince-andrews-excruciating-newsnight/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2019/11/17/queen-did-not-approve-prince-andrews-excruciating-newsnight/ |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=20 November 2019 |work=The Sunday Telegraph |date=17 November 2019}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Although Andrew was reportedly pleased with the outcome{{snd}}giving Maitlis and the ''Newsnight'' team a tour of Buckingham Palace<ref>{{cite news |last1=Waterson |first1=Jim |title='He was incredibly gracious after': Newsnight team say Andrew was pleased with interview |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/nov/17/meetings-rehearsals-and-approval-from-mum-newsnights-prince-andrew-coup |access-date=20 November 2019 |work=The Guardian|date=17 November 2019}}</ref>{{snd}}the interview received overwhelmingly negative reactions from the media and the public. It was described as a "car crash", "nuclear explosion level bad",<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/europe/prince-andrews-epstein-interview-roundly-panned-nuclear-explosion-level-bad/2019/11/17/62b28a02-094b-11ea-8054-289aef6e38a3_story.html|title=Prince Andrew's Epstein interview roundly panned: 'nuclear explosion level bad'|newspaper=The Washington Post|first=Karla|last=Adam|date=17 November 2019|access-date=17 November 2019|url-access=subscription|archive-date=17 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191117221753/https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/europe/prince-andrews-epstein-interview-roundly-panned-nuclear-explosion-level-bad/2019/11/17/62b28a02-094b-11ea-8054-289aef6e38a3_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2019/11/17/uk/prince-andrew-tv-interview-scli-gbr-intl/index.html|title=Prince Andrew sparks near-universal condemnation with TV interview|publisher=CNN|first=Aimee|last=Lewis|date=17 November 2019|access-date=17 November 2019|archive-date=17 November 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191117191953/https://edition.cnn.com/2019/11/17/uk/prince-andrew-tv-interview-scli-gbr-intl/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> and the worst [[public-relations]] crisis for the royal family since the [[death of Diana, Princess of Wales]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Landler |first1=Mark |title=After Disastrous Epstein Interview, Prince Andrew Steps Down From Public Duties |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/20/world/europe/prince-andrew-quits-epstein.html |access-date=20 November 2019 |work=The New York Times |date=20 November 2019 |url-access=limited |archive-date=17 June 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250617204326/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/20/world/europe/prince-andrew-quits-epstein.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Lawsuit ===
{{main|Giuffre v. Prince Andrew}}

In August 2021, Giuffre sued Andrew in the federal District Court for the Southern District of New York, accusing him of "sexual assault and intentional infliction of emotional distress".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walters |first1=Joanna |title=Epstein accuser Virginia Giuffre sues Prince Andrew |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2021/aug/09/virginia-giuffre-sues-prince-andrew-jeffrey-epstein |website=The Guardian |date=9 August 2021 |access-date=9 August 2021 |archive-date=27 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220127173855/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2021/aug/09/virginia-giuffre-sues-prince-andrew-jeffrey-epstein |url-status=live }}</ref> On 29 October 2021, Andrew's lawyers filed a response stating that he "unequivocally denies Giuffre's false allegations".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.edinburghlive.co.uk/news/uk-world-news/prince-andrew-unequivocally-denies-virginia-22018472|title=Prince Andrew 'unequivocally denies' Virginia Giuffre sexual assault claims|agency=PA Media|date=30 October 2021|website=edinburghlive|access-date=30 October 2021|archive-date=21 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211121021441/https://www.edinburghlive.co.uk/news/uk-world-news/prince-andrew-unequivocally-denies-virginia-22018472|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/10/29/world/prince-andrew-response-us-sexual-assault-case-intl/index.html|title=Prince Andrew's attorneys ask to dismiss US sex assault lawsuit saying it violates the terms of a settlement agreement|work=CNN|first=Sonia|last=Moghe|date=30 October 2021|access-date=30 October 2021|archive-date=16 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220116230729/https://www.cnn.com/2021/10/29/world/prince-andrew-response-us-sexual-assault-case-intl/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On 12 January 2022, Judge Kaplan rejected Andrew's attempts to dismiss the case, allowing the lawsuit to proceed.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jan/12/prince-andrew-case-proceed-latest-ruling-virginia-giuffre|title=Prince Andrew rejected in effort to get US sexual abuse case dismissed|date=12 January 2022|website=The Guardian|first=Victoria|last=Bekiempis|access-date=12 January 2022|archive-date=20 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220120235808/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jan/12/prince-andrew-case-proceed-latest-ruling-virginia-giuffre|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-59871514|title=Prince Andrew to face civil sex assault case after US ruling|date=12 January 2022|work=BBC|accessdate=12 January 2022|archive-date=16 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220116221629/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-59871514|url-status=live}}</ref> In February, the case was settled out of court, with Andrew making a donation to Giuffre's charity for victims of abuse, without any admission of liability.<ref>{{cite news |last=O'Connor |first=Mary |date=15 February 2022 |title=Prince Andrew settles US civil sex assault case |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-60393843 |access-date=15 February 2022 |website=BBC News |archive-date=15 February 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220215162320/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-60393843 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Repercussions ===
On 20 November 2019, Buckingham Palace announced that Andrew was suspending his public duties "for the foreseeable future". The decision, made with the Queen's consent, was accompanied by an insistence that Andrew sympathised with Epstein's victims.<ref>{{cite news|last=Quinn|first=Ben|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/nov/20/prince-andrew-to-step-back-from-public-duties-for-foreseeable-future|title=Prince Andrew to step back from public duties 'for foreseeable future'|work=[[The Guardian]]|location=London, England|date=20 November 2019|access-date=20 November 2019|archive-date=2 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210202233437/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/nov/20/prince-andrew-to-step-back-from-public-duties-for-foreseeable-future|url-status=live}}</ref> Other working royals took over his commitments in the short term.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-50498156|title=Prince Andrew seen for first time since stepping back from royal duties|website=BBC News|date=21 November 2019|access-date=21 November 2019|archive-date=7 April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250407174503/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-50498156|url-status=live}}</ref> On 24 November, the palace confirmed that Andrew would step down from all 230 of his patronages.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/nov/24/prince-andrew-to-withdraw-from-scores-of-charities|title=Prince Andrew to stand aside from all 230 of his patronages|newspaper=The Guardian|first=Robert|last=Booth|date=24 November 2019|access-date=24 November 2019}}</ref> The scrutiny of Andrew's relationship with Epstein also led to scrutiny of other public figures' ties to Epstein, notably the [[Relationship of Mette-Marit, Crown Princess of Norway, and Jeffrey Epstein|relationship of Mette-Marit, Crown Princess of Norway, and Epstein]].<ref name=nettavisenep>{{cite news|title=Epstein-filene: Omfattende kontakt med Mette-Marit|trans-title=Epstein files: Extensive contact with Mette-Marit|url=https://www.nettavisen.no/nyheter/epstein-filene-omfattende-kontakt-med-mette-marit/s/5-95-2855553|access-date=30 January 2026|work=Nettavisen|archive-date=30 January 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260130235352/https://www.nettavisen.no/nyheter/epstein-filene-omfattende-kontakt-med-mette-marit/s/5-95-2855553|url-status=live}}</ref>

In March 2020, Andrew hired Mark Gallagher, a crisis-management expert who had assisted high-profile clients falsely accused during [[Operation Midland]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2020/03/10/prince-andrew-hires-pr-man-advised-vips-falsely-accused-child/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2020/03/10/prince-andrew-hires-pr-man-advised-vips-falsely-accused-child/ |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live|title=Prince Andrew hires PR man who advised VIPs falsely accused of child sex abuse|newspaper=The Daily Telegraph|first1=Camilla|last1=Tominey|first2=Victoria|last2=Ward|date=10 March 2020|access-date=1 June 2020}}{{cbignore}}</ref> In May 2020, it was announced that Andrew would permanently resign from all public roles due to his ties to Epstein.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Nikkhah |first=Roya |date=21 May 2020 |title=Prince Andrew didn't think it was all over, but it is now |url=https://www.thetimes.com/article/prince-andrew-didn-t-think-it-was-all-over-but-it-is-now-rhg2trxb7 |url-access=subscription |access-date=20 July 2020 |work=[[The Times]] |archive-date=9 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250109160925/https://www.thetimes.com/article/prince-andrew-didn-t-think-it-was-all-over-but-it-is-now-rhg2trxb7 |url-status=live }}</ref> In January 2022, Andrew's social-media accounts were deleted, his page on the royal family's website was rewritten in the past tense, and his military affiliations and patronages were removed to emphasise his withdrawal from public life.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jan/19/prince-andrew-social-media-accounts-deleted-as-he-fights-us-lawsuit|title=Prince Andrew's social media accounts deleted as he fights US lawsuit|work=[[The Guardian]]|first=Caroline|last=Davies|date=19 January 2022|accessdate=19 January 2022}}</ref> He also stopped using the style [[His Royal Highness]] (HRH), although it was not formally removed.<ref name="BBC News-2022a" /> In June 2022, [[Rachael Maskell]], Labour MP for [[York Central (UK Parliament constituency)|York Central]], introduced a 'Removal of Titles' [[Private Members' Bills in the Parliament of the United Kingdom|private members bill]] in the House of Commons,<ref name="BBC News-2022b" /> which would have enabled the monarch or a parliamentary committee to strip aristocratic titles from individuals deemed unworthy.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.ie/world-news/europe/britain/prince-andrew-duke-of-york-and-dubious-peers-could-be-stripped-of-their-titles-41777630.html|title=Prince Andrew Duke of York and 'dubious' peers 'could be stripped of their titles'|work=[[Irish Independent]]|first=Martina|last=Bet|date=22 June 2022|accessdate=22 June 2022|archive-date=9 December 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221209191301/https://www.independent.ie/world-news/europe/britain/prince-andrew-duke-of-york-and-dubious-peers-could-be-stripped-of-their-titles-41777630.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="BBC News-2022b">{{cite news |date=21 June 2022 |title=Prince Andrew: Duke of York could lose title if bill becomes law |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-york-north-yorkshire-61880719 |accessdate=10 April 2024 |work=BBC News |archive-date=28 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250528035423/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-york-north-yorkshire-61880719 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In March 2022, Andrew made his first public appearance in months, helping the Queen walk into Westminster Abbey for a memorial service for his father, the Duke of Edinburgh.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Turner |first1=Lauren |title=Queen attends Prince Philip memorial service at Westminster Abbey |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-60902088 |work=BBC News |date=29 March 2022 |accessdate=29 March 2022 |archive-date=6 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220606155117/https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-60902088 |url-status=live }}</ref> In June 2022, Andrew took part in the private elements of the [[Garter Day]] ceremony, including lunch and the investiture of new members, but was excluded from the public procession amid reports that his brother Charles and nephew [[William, Prince of Wales|William]] had intervened to prevent him appearing in view of the public.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jun/13/prince-andrew-barred-from-procession-lobbing-charles-william-duke-of-york-garter-windsor |title=Prince Andrew misses Windsor Castle procession after 'family decision' |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=Caroline |last=Davies |date=13 June 2022 |accessdate=13 June 2022}}</ref> Following the [[death of Queen Elizabeth II]] on 8 September 2022, Andrew appeared in civilian clothing at various ceremonial events.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davies |first1=Caroline |title=Prince Andrew unlikely to resume royal duties under King Charles |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/sep/12/prince-andrew-unlikely-to-resume-royal-duties-king-charles-queen-role-state-funeral |access-date=13 September 2022 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=12 September 2022}}</ref> He wore military uniform for a 15-minute [[Vigil of the Princes|vigil beside the Queen's coffin]] at [[Westminster Hall]] on 16 September.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davies |first1=Caroline |title=King Charles to lead siblings in vigil over Queen's coffin in Westminster Hall |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/sep/15/king-charles-to-lead-siblings-in-vigil-over-queens-coffin-in-westminster-hall |access-date=16 September 2022 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=15 September 2022}}</ref>

In October 2022, it was reported that Andrew no longer received government funding.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tominey |first=Camilla |date=19 October 2022 |title=How Prince Andrew fills his days since his fall from grace |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/10/19/how-prince-andrew-fills-days-since-fall-grace/ |url-access=subscription |access-date=22 February 2023 |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |archive-date=28 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241228074144/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/10/19/how-prince-andrew-fills-days-since-fall-grace/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The following month, it was also reported that he was set to lose his police protection, as he was no longer expected to undertake public duties in line with King Charles III's wishes.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/prince-andrew-furious-after-losing-his-police-protection-zr5m3f0t7 |title=Prince Andrew 'furious' after losing his police protection |work=[[The Times]] |first=Dominic |last=Kennedy |date=28 November 2022 |access-date=30 November 2022 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=27 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250527191336/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/prince-andrew-furious-after-losing-his-police-protection-zr5m3f0t7 |url-status=live }}</ref> His armed personal protection officers were expected to be replaced by private security guards, likely to be funded by the King, at an estimated cost of up to £3 million per year.<ref name="Ward-2022">{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/12/19/king-charles-pay-duke-yorks-private-security-refusing-do-prince/ |title=King Charles to likely pay for Prince Andrew's private security – after refusing to do so for Harry |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |first=Victoria |last=Ward |date=19 December 2022 |accessdate=19 December 2022 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=15 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250515054755/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2022/12/19/king-charles-pay-duke-yorks-private-security-refusing-do-prince/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In January 2023, it was reported that Andrew could no longer use his suite of rooms at Buckingham Palace.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-andrew-king-charles-buckingham-palace-b2269728.html |title=King Charles 'tells Prince Andrew there is no place' for him at Buckingham Palace |work=[[The Independent]] |first=Kate |last=Ng |date=27 January 2023 |accessdate=28 January 2023 |archive-date=5 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250305194027/https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-andrew-king-charles-buckingham-palace-b2269728.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In August 2024, ''The Telegraph'' reported that the King would withdraw funding for Andrew's security by the end of October, requiring him to pay for future security operations at Royal Lodge.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2024/08/18/king-axes-duke-york-security-team-royal-lodge-andrew-prince/ |title=King axes Duke of York's security team |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |first=Victoria |last=Ward |date=18 August 2024 |accessdate=18 August 2024 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=20 January 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250120151056/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2024/08/18/king-axes-duke-york-security-team-royal-lodge-andrew-prince/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

{{Further|#Titles and styles}}
On 2 November 2025, Defence Secretary [[John Healey]] confirmed that Andrew's [[Vice-admiral (Royal Navy)|honorary rank of vice-admiral]]{{snd}}retained after he relinquished his other military titles in 2022{{snd}}would be removed following direction from the King,<ref>Kiran Stacey, [https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/nov/02/andrew-to-be-stripped-of-naval-title-says-uk-defence-secretary Andrew to be stripped of naval title, says UK defence secretary], ''The Guardian'' (2 November 2025).</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Nanji |first1=Noor |last2=Kuenssberg |first2=Laura |date=2 November 2025 |title=King wants Andrew to lose final military title, minister says |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8675l25591o |access-date=2 November 2025 |work=[[BBC News]]}}</ref> a process finalised by 13 December.<ref name=":1" /> He continues to hold the [[South Atlantic Medal]] with rosette, awarded to all who served in the Falklands War.<ref>{{cite news|first=Caroline|last=Davies|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/nov/05/andrew-keep-falklands-medal-losing-royal-military-titles|title=Andrew allowed to keep Falklands medal despite losing royal and military titles|work=The Guardian|date=5 November 2025}}</ref> On 3 November, [[Letters patent (United Kingdom)|letters patent]] were issued removing Andrew's style of "[[Royal Highness]]" and the title "prince";<ref name="Gazette-Removals">{{Cite web |date=3 November 2025 |title=Crown Office |url=https://www.thegazette.co.uk/notice/4992102 |access-date=23 February 2026 |website=[[The London Gazette]] |publication-date=5 November 2025}}</ref> without these honorifics, it was agreed that he would use the family surname [[Mountbatten-Windsor]].<ref name=":2" /> His appointments to the [[Royal Victorian Order]] and [[Order of the Garter]] were also rescinded,<ref name="LG" /> and his banner was removed from [[St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle]], the chapel of the Order of the Garter.<ref name=banner>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c867j2wyxj0o|title=Prince Andrew's banner removed from chapel at Windsor Castle|work=BBC News|date=24 October 2025|author=Kathryn Armstrong|archive-date=4 December 2025|access-date=24 October 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251204012225/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c867j2wyxj0o|url-status=live}}</ref>

Later that month, Andrew's life membership of the [[Savage Club]] was withdrawn.<ref>{{cite news |author=Hope |first=Christopher |date=14 November 2025 |title=Andrew savaged in clubland |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/11/14/andrew-savaged-in-clubland/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251115185304/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/11/14/andrew-savaged-in-clubland/ |archive-date=15 November 2025 |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]}}</ref> Commemorative plaques bearing his name were removed from several locations in the [[Falkland Islands]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wootton-Cane |first1=Nicole |last2=Gooch |first2=Bryony |date=17 November 2025 |title=Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor's name stripped from Falklands plaques |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-falklands-epstein-latest-b2866301.html |access-date=19 November 2025 |work=[[The Independent]] |archive-date=17 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251117235431/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-falklands-epstein-latest-b2866301.html |url-status=live }}</ref> and Mid and East Antrim Council agreed to rename Prince Andrew Way in [[Carrickfergus]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Mid and East Antrim Council agrees to rename 'Prince Andrew Way' |url=https://www.rte.ie/news/ireland/2025/1124/1545658-prince-andrew-way/ |work=[[RTÉ News]] |date=24 November 2025 |access-date=24 November 2025 |archive-date=24 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251124223448/https://www.rte.ie/news/ireland/2025/1124/1545658-prince-andrew-way/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On 19 November, [[Metropolitan Police]] firearms-licensing officers requested that Andrew voluntarily surrender his firearms and shotgun certificate, which he did. No reason for the surrender was given.<ref>{{cite news |last=Aikman |first=Ian |date=23 December 2025 |title=Andrew gives up gun licence after police request |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5yde5p9kw9o |access-date=24 December 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |archive-date=23 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251223175727/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5yde5p9kw9o |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 6 May 2026, Alex Jenkinson, a 39‑year‑old man from Suffolk, was charged by [[Norfolk Police]] with threatening and abusive behaviour after allegedly intimidating and threatening Andrew while he was walking his dogs near his home on the Sandringham Estate, prompting Andrew{{Spaced en dash}}who was reportedly accompanied by private security{{Spaced en dash}}to leave the area by car as the suspect allegedly chased after him.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/may/07/man-arrested-near-home-of-andrew-mountbatten-windsor|title=Man charged after allegedly threatening Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor|work=The Guardian|first=Caroline|last=Davies|date=7 May 2026|access-date=8 May 2026}}</ref> It was further reported that Jenkinson had researched the [[Catherine, Princess of Wales|Princess of Wales]] and her children, as well as looking up weapons and methods of killing.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2026/06/18/andrew-stalker-princess-charlotte-and-killing-methods/|title=Andrew stalker researched Princess Charlotte and killing methods|work=The Daily Telegraph|first=Martin|last=Evans|date=18 June 2026|access-date=20 June 2026|url-access=subscription|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/ph35Y|archive-date=19 June 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Misconduct in public office ===
Emails disclosed as part of the Epstein files in early 2026 appear to indicate that, between 2010 and 2011, Andrew may have knowingly shared confidential information with Epstein about his official work as trade envoy; trade envoys have a duty of confidentiality over sensitive, commercial, or political information arising from their official visits.<ref>{{cite news |title=Andrew shared confidential information with Epstein as trade envoy, files suggest |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c99j01p1yjro |work=BBC News |first=Andy |last=Verity |access-date=9 February 2026 |date=8 February 2026 |archive-date=8 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260208203310/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c99j01p1yjro |url-status=live }}</ref> It is further alleged that in 2010, Andrew passed on an email conversation about the [[Royal Bank of Scotland]] and [[Aston Martin]] to Terence Allen and [[David Stern (German businessman)|David Stern]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Andrew's time as trade envoy should be investigated, says Vince Cable |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cp32j7v2dy5o |website=[[BBC News]] |access-date=15 February 2026 |date=15 February 2026}}</ref> In February 2026, [[Thames Valley Police]] stated that they were considering investigating [[Republic (pressure group)|Republic]]'s report concerning Andrew for suspected [[misconduct in public office]] and an alleged breach of official secrets.<ref name="BBC cqlgv3z79klo2">{{cite news |last1=Coughlan |first1=Sean |title=King 'ready to support' police as they assess Andrew claims over Epstein |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqlgv3z79klo |access-date=19 February 2026 |work=[[BBC News]] |date=9 February 2026 |archive-date=12 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260212002058/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqlgv3z79klo |url-status=live }}</ref> In the [[United Kingdom]], misconduct in public office is a [[Common law offence|common-law crime]] not subject to a [[statute of limitations]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nationalreview.com/corner/no-statute-of-limitations-in-former-prince-andrews-public-corruption-case/|title=No Statute of Limitations in Former Prince Andrew's Public Corruption Case|work=National Review|first1=Andrew|last1=McCarthy|date=19 February 2026|access-date=23 February 2026}}</ref> 

In May 2026, it was reported that a search of court documents had shown that Buckingham Palace had received an email archive six years earlier that would have indicated Andrew shared confidential government information while serving as trade envoy.<ref name="BBC-BPem">{{cite news |author=Coughlan |first=Sean |last2=King |first2=Ben |date=30 May 2026 |title=Palace was handed Andrew's controversial envoy emails six years ago |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cy02j5pl98no |access-date=30 May 2026 |work=BBC News}}</ref> The filings state that about 30,000 emails, containing material linked to his disputed financial dealings, were passed to the [[Lord Chamberlain]] in 2020 after being taken from one of Andrew's business associates.<ref name="BBC-BPem"/> Buckingham Palace said it could not comment while a police inquiry involving him was ongoing.<ref name="BBC-BPem"/>

==== Arrest ====
On the morning of 19 February 2026, his 66th birthday, Andrew was arrested on suspicion of misconduct in public office at the Sandringham estate,<ref name="BBC c70kjr9wjw0t 11182">{{cite news |date=19 February 2026 |title=Andrew released under investigation after arrest on suspicion of misconduct in public office |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/live/c70kjr9wjw0t |access-date=19 February 2026 |work=[[BBC News]]}}</ref> where he had been living since leaving his home in Windsor.<ref name="BBC c70kjr9wjw0t 11182" /> It was the first arrest of a senior member of the British royal family since [[Charles I of England#Captivity|that of King Charles I]] in 1647,<ref name="Guardian-Arrest" /> and the first arrest of a sibling of the reigning monarch since [[Elizabeth I#Heir_presumptive|that of Elizabeth I]] in 1554.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-former-prince-andrew-arrested-epstein/|title=Former prince Andrew arrested on suspicion of misconduct in public office related to Epstein ties|first=Paul|last=Waldie|date=19 February 2026|access-date=19 February 2026|work=[[The Globe and Mail]]}}</ref>

[[Thames Valley Police]] stated that they were searching addresses in Berkshire and [[Norfolk]], and it was subsequently confirmed that officers were searching Royal Lodge in [[Windsor Great Park]], where Andrew previously lived.<ref name="BBC c70kjr9wjw0t 11182" /> Charles III released a statement expressing his "deepest concern", and said that "the law must take its course".<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 February 2026 |title=A Statement from His Majesty The King |url=https://www.royal.uk/news-and-activity/2026-02-19/a-statement-from-his-majesty-the-king |access-date=19 February 2026 |work=The Royal Family}}</ref> Andrew was released from [[Aylsham]] police station later that day, around 11 hours after his arrest, under investigation.<ref name="BBC c70kjr9wjw0t 11182" /><ref name="Guardian-Arrest">{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/live/2026/feb/19/police-arrest-former-prince-andrew-mountbatten-windsor-sandringham-latest-updates|title=Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor released under investigation after arrest – as it happened|work=The Guardian|date=19 February 2026|accessdate=19 February 2026}}</ref>

====Investigation====
Metropolitan Police officials have sought the cooperation of the United States Department of Justice in relation to the investigation into Andrew. Communication between the UK and US authorities is regarded as an initial step before any formal requests are made, including [[mutual legal assistance treaty]] (MLAT) requests.<ref>{{cite web |author1=Erica Orden|title=British authorities seek DOJ's help in investigations into former Prince Andrew, Peter Mandelson|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/03/18/epstein-files-andrew-mountbatten-windsor-peter-mandelson-00835334 |website=[[Politico]] |access-date=19 March 2026 |date=18 March 2026}}</ref> [[Sir Mark Rowley]], Commissioner of the Metropolitan Police, said that the force is assessing a "whole range of sexual allegations" concerning Andrew to determine whether they "merit a criminal investigation".<ref>{{cite news|author1=Steven Swinford|title=Met investigates bailout email from Peter Mandelson to Jeffrey Epstein|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/crime/article/scotland-yard-peter-mandelson-investigation-jeffrey-epstein-eurozone-email-qtrdpdwph |website=[[The Times]] |access-date=19 March 2026 |date=18 March 2026}}{{subscription required}}</ref>

====Reuters photograph====
[[File:Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor release from arrest photograph.png|thumb|Widely circulated photograph by [[Phil Noble (photographer)|Phil Noble]] of Andrew leaving custody]]
A [[Reuters]] photograph by [[Phil Noble (photographer)|Phil Noble]], showing what BBC News described as a "shell-shocked, haunted" Andrew slumping in his car as he left the police station, attracted international attention.<ref name="CoughlanAndrewPhoto">{{cite news |last=Coughlan |first=Sean |date=19 February 2026 |title=Shell-shocked, haunted photo of Andrew will be part of how arrest is remembered |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/czr0vj13ezjo |access-date=22 February 2026 |work=BBC News}}</ref><ref name="RNZReutersAndrewPhoto">{{cite news |date=20 February 2026 |title=How Reuters captured the photo of former prince Andrew leaving custody |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/world/587453/how-reuters-captured-the-photo-of-former-prince-andrew-leaving-custody |access-date=22 February 2026 |work=RNZ}}</ref> The image was briefly hung at the [[Louvre]] by activists from [[Everyone Hates Elon]] under the title ''He's Sweating Now''{{Spaced en dash}}a reference to Andrew's widely publicised claim in his 2019 ''Newsnight'' interview that he had been unable to sweat at the time of the alleged events<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Hatcher |first=Kirsty |date=20 February 2026 |title=Virginia Giuffre's family says ex‑Prince Andrew is 'sweating now' following his release from custody |url=https://people.com/virginia-giuffre-family-ex-prince-andrew-sweating-now-after-release-from-custody-arrest-11910446 |access-date=24 February 2026 |work=People}}</ref>{{Spaced en dash}} before being removed by museum staff after approximately 15 minutes.<ref>{{cite news |last=Stout |first=Amelia |date=23 February 2026 |title=Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor post-arrest picture hung in the Louvre |url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/andrew-mountbatten-windsor-post-arrest-picture-hung-in-the-louvre-bnpst959d |access-date=23 February 2026 |work=The Times}}</ref>

=== Allegations of being a Russian asset ===
In February 2026, the ''Daily Mail'' reported that a 2025 United States security service assessment, titled ''Political Corruption: Andrew Mountbatten‑Windsor: Designated Russian Union'', had been leaked to the newspaper. According to the report, the [[Russian government]] had targeted Andrew through Epstein as a way to infiltrate Britain and described him as a "useful idiot" and the "weak link" in the royal family. It stated that Russian agents exploited his "long‑standing hatred of his brother Charles" and that he "was not blackmailed or otherwise coerced into this role – rather he was a willing participant in these schemes due to financial, sexual and personal reward". The assessment further said that "Russian intelligence used AMW's sexual proclivities to establish a relationship rapport in order to exploit this mental condition for further gain".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Doyle |first1=Liam |title=Andrew 'targeted by Russian spymasters' claim secret documents |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/royals/andrew-targeted-russian-spymasters-claim-36789619 |access-date=17 May 2026 |work=Daily Mirror |date=27 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260227103743/https://www.mirror.co.uk/news/royals/andrew-targeted-russian-spymasters-claim-36789619 |archive-date=27 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Henrys |first1=Rebecca |title=Andrew 'targeted by Russian spies' who exploited his 'hatred' of Charles |url=https://www.lbc.co.uk/article/andrew-russia-epstein-charles-5HjdTcC_2/ |access-date=17 May 2026 |work=LBC |date=27 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260227114931/https://www.lbc.co.uk/article/andrew-russia-epstein-charles-5HjdTcC_2/ |archive-date=27 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sykes |first1=Tom |title=U.S. Spies' Verdict on Prince Andrew Outed in Jaw-Dropping Report |url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/weak-link-andrew-let-sibling-rivalry-fuel-russian-interference/ |access-date=17 May 2026 |work=The Daily Beast |date=27 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

Later that year, Andrew's biographer [[Andrew Lownie]] claimed that "Epstein was a Soviet asset" and alleged that Andrew's relationship with him involved "huge national security scandal [...] of penetration". Lownie said that, in his view, the palace preferred to "keep it to the sexual side – everyone understands that bit – and certainly not go anywhere near the national security scandal" and that "the plan [of the palace], I think, at the moment, is to throw Andrew to the wolves".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Williams |first1=Zoe |title='We've been paying for happy endings for Andrew for years': the inside story of a royal disgrace, by his biographer |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/feb/24/andrew-mountbatten-windsor-biographer-andrew-lownie-entitled |access-date=17 May 2026 |work=The Guardian |date=24 February 2026}}</ref>

== Other allegations ==

=== Racist language ===
Rohan Silva, a former Downing Street aide, claimed that, when they met in 2012, Andrew had commented, "Well, if you'll pardon the expression, that really is the [[nigger in the woodpile]]."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2019/nov/18/prince-andrew-used-n-word-former-no-10-aide-claims|title=Prince Andrew used the N-word, former No 10 aide claims|last=Waterson|first=Jim|date=18 November 2019|via=www.theguardian.com|newspaper=The Guardian}}</ref> The former home secretary [[Jacqui Smith]] claimed that Andrew made a racist comment about [[Arabs]] during a state dinner for the [[Saudi royal family]] in 2007.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davies |first1=Gareth |title=Prince Andrew made 'racist' comment about Arabs, ex-home secretary says |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2019/11/19/politician-breaks-ranks-label-prince-andrew-complete-disgrace/ |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220110/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/royal-family/2019/11/19/politician-breaks-ranks-label-prince-andrew-complete-disgrace/ |archive-date=10 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |newspaper=The Telegraph |access-date=19 November 2019|date=19 November 2019 }}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Neill |first1=Sean |title=Prince Andrew was racist about Arabs at state banquet, claims former home secretary Jacqui Smith |url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/crime/article/prince-andrew-was-racist-about-arabs-at-state-banquet-claims-former-home-secretary-jacqui-smith-wrbqjgzxw |newspaper=[[The Times]] |access-date=19 November 2019 |date=19 November 2019 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=27 August 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250827033244/https://www.thetimes.com/uk/crime/article/prince-andrew-was-racist-about-arabs-at-state-banquet-claims-former-home-secretary-jacqui-smith-wrbqjgzxw |url-status=live }}</ref> Buckingham Palace denied that Andrew had used racist language on either occasion.<ref>{{Cite news| url=https://www.theaustralian.com.au/world/the-times/former-british-home-secretary-accuses-prince-andrew-of-racism/news-story/6c9de3b5dab38bc6e64487377332b810 |title = Former British home secretary accuses Prince Andrew of racism | url-access=subscription | website=The Australian}}</ref>

=== Treatment of others ===
During his four-day tour of [[Southern California]] in 1984, Andrew squirted paint at American and British journalists and photographers who were reporting on the visit, after which he told Los Angeles county supervisor [[Kenneth Hahn]], "I enjoyed that."<ref name="Childress-1984">{{cite web|url=https://www.upi.com/Archives/1984/04/24/Crown-may-pay-for-cameras-clothes-sprayed-by-prince/1107451630800/|title=Crown may pay for cameras, clothes sprayed by prince|work=UPI|first=Deirdre|last=Childress|date=24 April 1984|accessdate=25 January 2022|archive-date=24 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250624020625/https://www.upi.com/Archives/1984/04/24/Crown-may-pay-for-cameras-clothes-sprayed-by-prince/1107451630800/|url-status=live}}</ref> The incident damaged reporters' clothing and equipment, and the ''[[Los Angeles Herald Examiner]]'' submitted a $1,200 bill to the [[List of diplomatic missions of the United Kingdom#Americas|British consulate]] seeking compensation.<ref name="Childress-1984" />

''[[The Guardian]]'' wrote in 2022 that "his brusque manner with servants is well-documented. A senior [[footman]] once told a reporter who worked undercover at Buckingham Palace that on waking Andrew "the response can easily be 'fuck off' as 'good morning{{'"}}.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jan/08/royals-await-anxiously-the-fallout-from-prince-andrews-disgrace|title=Royals await anxiously the fallout from Prince Andrew's disgrace|date=8 January 2022|accessdate=9 January 2022|work=The Guardian|first=Andrew|last=Anthony}}</ref> Former royal protection officer Paul Page, who himself was convicted and given a six-year prison sentence following a £3 million property investment scam in 2009,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/paul-page-royal-officer-guilty-fraud-b1995823.html|title=Paul Page: The former royal protection officer found guilty of fraud|work=The Independent|first=Laurie|last=Churchman|date=19 January 2022|accessdate=25 January 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2009/jul/17/royal-protection-officer-fraud-guilty|title=Former royal protection officer guilty of £3m scam|work=The Guardian|first=Sandra|last=Laville|date=17 July 2009|accessdate=25 January 2022}}</ref> said in an [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] documentary that Andrew kept a collection of "50 or 60 stuffed toys" and would "shout and scream and become verbally abusive" if they "weren't put back in the right order by the maids".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/ghislaine-maxwell-itv-page-prince-two-b1994572.html|title=Andrew's teddy bear collection was not to be disturbed, claims documentary|work=The Independent|date=17 January 2022|accessdate=17 January 2022|archive-date=13 March 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250313032141/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/ghislaine-maxwell-itv-page-prince-two-b1994572.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Page later alleged in the documentary ''Prince Andrew: Banished'' that different women visited Andrew daily, and that when one was denied entry by security, Andrew allegedly called an officer a "fat, lardy cunt" over the phone. Page said of Andrew: "He's a bully."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.the-independent.com/news/world/americas/prince-andrew-jeffrey-epstein-sex-trafficking-b2199354.html|title=New documentary takes aim at disgraced Prince Andrew: 'Runt of the litter' and 'idiot'|work=The Independent|first=Sheila|last=Flynn|date=10 October 2022|access-date=25 October 2025|archive-date=2 January 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260102062538/https://www.the-independent.com/news/world/americas/prince-andrew-jeffrey-epstein-sex-trafficking-b2199354.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

Andrew was also alleged to have damaged sensor-operated gates in [[Windsor Great Park]] by forcing them open in his [[Range Rover]] to avoid going an extra mile on his way home.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2016/mar/16/duke-of-york-should-be-prosecuted-for-ramming-open-park-gate|title=Duke of York 'should be prosecuted for ramming open park gate'|work=The Guardian|first=Matthew|last=Weaver|date=16 March 2016|accessdate=24 January 2022}}</ref> In March 2016, the [[Thames Valley Police]] dismissed the formal report by [[Republic (political organisation)|Republic]] due to lack of details.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2016/mar/17/prince-andrew-reported-to-police-for-ramming-windsor-great-park-gates|title=Police won't investigate Prince Andrew over alleged gate ramming|work=The Guardian|first=Matthew|last=Weaver|date=17 March 2016|accessdate=24 January 2022}}</ref>

Andrew's former maid, Charlotte Briggs, also recalled arranging the teddy bears on his bed and told ''[[The Sun (United Kingdom)|The Sun]]'' that when she was bitten by his [[Norfolk Terrier]] in 1996 he "wasn't bothered". Briggs said that a butler later told her that Andrew and his personal doctor "laughed" about the incident.<ref name="Cartwright-2022">{{cite web|url=https://www.news.com.au/entertainment/celebrity-life/royals/former-palace-maid-spills-on-prince-andrews-entitled-demands/news-story/de6a4f333b55090f4e154915d0258d34|title=Former palace maid spills on Prince Andrew's 'entitled' demands|website=news.com.au|first=Lexie|last=Cartwright|date=22 January 2022|accessdate=25 January 2022|archive-date=8 January 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250108163916/https://www.news.com.au/entertainment/celebrity-life/royals/former-palace-maid-spills-on-prince-andrews-entitled-demands/news-story/de6a4f333b55090f4e154915d0258d34|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Hanna-2022">{{cite web|url=https://www.womanandhome.com/us/life/royal-news/prince-andrew-laughed-when-his-dog-bit-maid-leaving-her-bleeding/|title=Prince Andrew laughed when his dog bit maid, leaving her 'bleeding'|work=Woman & Home|first=Aoife|last=Hanna|date=21 January 2022|accessdate=25 January 2022|archive-date=8 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220608030655/https://www.womanandhome.com/us/life/royal-news/prince-andrew-laughed-when-his-dog-bit-maid-leaving-her-bleeding/|url-status=live}}</ref> Andrew's dog was subsequently banned from Buckingham Palace.<ref name="Hanna-2022"/> Briggs said she had been reduced to tears after being reprimanded for not properly closing the heavy curtains in his office, adding that his behaviour contrasted with that of his brothers Charles and Edward, who "weren't anything like him" and his father Philip, whom she described as "so nice and gentlemanly".<ref name="Cartwright-2022" />

Emma Gruenbaum, a massage therapist, told ''The Sun'' that Andrew regularly overstepped boundaries, making sexual comments during appointments. She alleged he talked continually about sex during their first session and asked about when she had last had sex. Gruenbaum said Andrew arranged regular massages for around two months, and she believed the requests stopped when he realised he would not get more.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-royal-massage-physiotherapist-b2012935.html|title=Prince Andrew 'was constant sex pest' to massage therapist|work=[[The Independent]]|first=Chiara|last=Giordano|date=11 February 2022|accessdate=12 February 2022|archive-date=20 June 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250620000756/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/home-news/prince-andrew-royal-massage-physiotherapist-b2012935.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2025, Andrew Lownie alleged in his book ''[[Entitled: The Rise and Fall of the House of York]]'' that Andrew reprimanded a palace employee for not using the proper name and title when referring to his grandmother [[Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother]], calling him a "fucking imbecile".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/aug/06/key-takeaways-from-explosive-claims-made-in-biography-on-prince-andrew|title=Key takeaways from explosive claims made in biography of Prince Andrew|work=The Guardian|first=Caroline|last=Davies|date=6 August 2025|access-date=8 August 2025}}</ref> Another employee alleged that Andrew would "explode one minute and then try to take it back the next".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/culture/books/article/entitled-rise-fall-house-york-andrew-lownie-review-prince-andrew-n3gjpf5sc|title=The life of Prince Andrew is 'a black comedy and a tragedy'|work=The Times|first=Kate|last=Mansey|date=8 August 2025|access-date=8 August 2025|archive-date=23 September 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250923192139/https://www.thetimes.com/culture/books/article/entitled-rise-fall-house-york-andrew-lownie-review-prince-andrew-n3gjpf5sc|url-status=live}}</ref> Biographer [[Robert Hardman]] alleged in ''[[Elizabeth II: In Private. In Public. The Inside Story]]'' that Andrew had struck the [[Master of the Household]], [[Tony Johnstone-Burt]], during a dispute over room availability for one of Andrew's events, and that the incident led to Prince Philip sending Johnstone‑Burt a letter of apology.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.hellomagazine.com/royalty/896288/queen-elizabeths-grand-gesture-to-royal-aide-after-andrew-mountbatten-windsor-got-physical/|title=Queen Elizabeth's grand gesture to royal aide after Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor got physical|magazine=Hello!|first=Katherine|last=Robinson|date=17 April 2026|access-date=19 April 2026}}</ref>

In May 2026, it was reported that Thames Valley Police were examining allegations that Andrew had acted inappropriately towards a waitress at [[Royal Ascot]] in June 2002.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2026/05/24/andrew-allegation-inappropriate-behaviour-royal-ascot/|title=Andrew accused of 'inappropriate behaviour' at Royal Ascot|work=The Telegraph|first=Will|last=Bolton|date=24 May 2026|access-date=25 May 2026|url-access=subscription|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/by9QM|archive-date=2 June 2026|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/royal-family/article/andrew-mountbatten-windsor-royal-ascot-waitress-7g8n2rp53|title=Woman at centre of fresh Andrew allegation was Royal Ascot waitress|work=The Times|date=30 May 2026|access-date=1 June 2026|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

==Titles, styles, honours and arms==

===Titles and styles===
[[File:Royal Monogram Of Prince Andrew Of Great Britain.svg|thumb|right|upright=0.4|Monogram]]
As a son of the reigning monarch, Andrew was styled at birth as "His [[Royal Highness]] The Prince Andrew".<ref name=Titles/> On 23 July 1986, the day of his wedding, he was created [[Duke of York]], [[Earl of Inverness]], and [[Baron Killyleagh]], and assumed the style "His Royal Highness The Duke of York". He was occasionally known as Earl of Inverness in Scotland and Baron Killyleagh in [[Northern Ireland]].<ref name=Titles>{{cite web|url=http://www.royal.gov.uk/ThecurrentRoyalFamily/TheDukeofYork/Styleandtitles.aspx |title=The Duke of York – Style and titles |publisher=royal.gov.uk |access-date=25 April 2015 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150505193349/http://www.royal.gov.uk/ThecurrentRoyalFamily/TheDukeofYork/Styleandtitles.aspx |archive-date=5 May 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Coke |first=Hope |url=https://www.tatler.com/gallery/royal-family-regional-titles |title=A comprehensive guide to the Royal Family's regional titles |date=20 May 2021 |magazine=[[Tatler]] |access-date=27 May 2021 |archive-date=21 September 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230921110807/https://www.tatler.com/gallery/royal-family-regional-titles |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.townandcountrymag.com/society/tradition/g26783028/british-royal-family-full-titles-names/ |title=A Guide to Royal Family Titles, from the Queen's Specific Styling to Prince Harry's Scottish Moniker |date=6 June 2019 |work=Town & Country |access-date=27 May 2021 |archive-date=19 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240219190030/https://www.townandcountrymag.com/society/tradition/g26783028/british-royal-family-full-titles-names/ |url-status=live }}</ref>[[File:Procession to Lying-in-State of Elizabeth II at Westminster Hall - 55.jpg|thumb|Procession from [[Buckingham Palace]] to [[Westminster Hall]] for the [[Death and state funeral of Elizabeth II|lying-in-state of Queen Elizabeth II]] on 14 September 2022]]
In 2019, in light of Andrew's friendship with the convicted sex offender Epstein, residents of [[Inverness]] began a campaign to strip him of his [[earl]]dom, saying that "it is inappropriate that Prince Andrew is associated with our beautiful city".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.inverness-courier.co.uk/news/petition-points-to-exit-for-prince-andrew-186645/ |title=Petition urges Queen to strip Prince Andrew of Earl of Inverness title |work=Inverness Courier |date=21 November 2019 |access-date=31 May 2020 |archive-date=9 December 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241209035810/https://www.inverness-courier.co.uk/news/petition-points-to-exit-for-prince-andrew-186645/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==== 2022 ====
On 13 January 2022, Buckingham Palace announced that Andrew had ceased using the style "Royal Highness" publicly,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Quinn |first=Ben |last2=Davies |first2=Caroline |date=13 January 2022 |title=Prince Andrew loses military roles and use of HRH title |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jan/13/veterans-ask-queen-to-strip-prince-andrew-of-honorary-military-titles |access-date=7 July 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref><ref name="BBC News-2022a" /><ref>{{cite news |date=16 February 2022 |title=Prince Andrew: Who is he and what titles is he losing? |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/explainers-59993936 |work=BBC News |archive-date=18 June 2022 |access-date=20 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220618125143/https://www.bbc.com/news/explainers-59993936 |url-status=live }}</ref> though he remained permitted to use it privately.<ref name="Ward-2023" /> On 14 January, [[Rachael Maskell]], the [[Labour Co-op]] MP for [[York Central (UK Parliament constituency)|York Central]], called for Andrew to lose his [[Duke|dukedom]], and a renewed petition to remove Andrew's earldom was launched in Inverness, gaining over 6000 signatures by 16 September.<ref>{{cite news |last=Meighan |first=Craig |date=16 September 2022 |title=More than 6000 sign petition calling for Prince Andrew to lose Earl of Inverness title |url=https://www.thenational.scot/news/21869302.6000-sign-petition-calling-prince-andrew-lose-earl-inverness-title/ |access-date=17 September 2022 |work=[[The National (Scotland)|The National]] |archive-date=24 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250224145124/https://www.thenational.scot/news/21869302.6000-sign-petition-calling-prince-andrew-lose-earl-inverness-title/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 16 February, residents of [[Killyleagh]] expressed their opinion that Andrew should be stripped of his [[Baron|barony]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Cochrane |first=Amy |date=16 February 2022 |title=Prince Andrew: Killyleagh townsfolk want their Baron to be stripped of his title |url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/news/northern-ireland/prince-andrew-killyleagh-townsfolk-want-their-baron-to-be-stripped-of-his-title/41354274.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251018083730/https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/news/northern-ireland/prince-andrew-killyleagh-townsfolk-want-their-baron-to-be-stripped-of-his-title/41354274.html |archive-date= 2025-10-18|access-date=19 October 2025 |work=[[Belfast Telegraph]] |language=en-GB |issn=0307-1235|url-status=live}}</ref> and Maskell said she would seek legislation to remove Andrew's dukedom if he did not relinquish it voluntarily.<ref>{{cite news|last=Halliday |first=Josh |date=16 February 2022 |title=Prince Andrew facing fresh calls to be stripped of Duke of York title |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/feb/16/prince-andrew-facing-fresh-calls-stripped-duke-of-york-title-virginia-giuffre |accessdate=17 February 2022 |work=The Guardian}}</ref> On 27 April, the [[City of York Council]] removed Andrew's honorary freedom of the city by a unanimous vote, and York councillors called for him to lose his dukedom.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-andrew-duke-york-city-b2067451.html|title=Will Prince Andrew still be the Duke of York?|work=[[The Independent]]|first=Kate|last=Ng|date=28 April 2022|accessdate=29 April 2022|archive-date=24 December 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241224232941/https://www.independent.co.uk/life-style/royal-family/prince-andrew-duke-york-city-b2067451.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

====2025 changes====
On 17 October 2025, following discussions with King Charles III, Andrew agreed to cease using his peerages and honours, including his dukedom and his knighthoods as a [[Royal Knight Companion of the Order of the Garter]] and a [[Knight Grand Cross of the Royal Victorian Order]].<ref>{{cite news |date=17 October 2025 |last1=Coughlan |first1=Sean |title=Prince Andrew gives up his title as Duke of York |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgqlyw9g7weo |access-date=18 October 2025 |work=BBC News |archive-date=29 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251129230242/https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cgqlyw9g7weo |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=17 October 2025 |title=A statement by Prince Andrew |url=https://www.royal.uk/news-and-activity/2025-10-17/a-statement-by-prince-andrew |access-date=17 October 2025 |website=Royal.uk |publisher=The Royal Household}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Davies |first1=Caroline |title=Prince Andrew gives up royal titles including Duke of York after 'discussion with king' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/oct/17/prince-andrew-to-give-up-royal-titles |access-date=18 October 2025 |work=The Guardian |date=17 October 2025}}</ref> His [[Royal standard of the United Kingdom#Other members of the royal family|banner of arms]], which had hung in [[St George's Chapel, Windsor Castle]], since 2006 to signify his membership of the Order of the Garter, was removed.<ref name=banner/>

On 30 October, Buckingham Palace announced that the King had begun a "formal process" to remove his brother's style, titles, and honours.<ref name="Nanji-2025" /> Andrew's name was removed from the [[Roll of the Peerage]] the same day.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.college-of-arms.gov.uk/images/downloads/Roll_of_the_Peerage.pdf|title=Roll of the Peerage|work=College of Arms|access-date=30 October 2025|archive-date=31 October 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251031094117/https://www.college-of-arms.gov.uk/images/downloads/Roll_of_the_Peerage.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=30 October 2025 |title=Warrants Under the Royal Sign Manual |url=https://www.thegazette.co.uk/notice/4992105 |access-date=23 February 2026 |website=[[The London Gazette]] |publication-date=5 November 2025}}</ref> Although this did not revoke his peerages,<ref name="hoclib">{{Cite web |last=Torrance |first=David |date=1 December 2025 |title=The removal of titles and honours |url=https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10370/ |access-date=3 December 2025 |website=[[House of Commons Library]]|quote=On 4 November 2025, Nick Thomas-Symonds (Minister for the Cabinet Office) stated in response to a parliamentary question that: "An Act of Parliament is required to remove a peerage once conferred".}}</ref> it meant he was no longer entitled to any place in the [[orders of precedence in the United Kingdom]] derived from them, and ceased to be addressed or referred to by any title derived from his peerages in official documents.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=The Royal Warrant of 1 June 2004 |url=https://www.college-of-arms.gov.uk/RoyalWarrant2004.pdf |access-date=31 October 2025 |website=[[College of Arms]] |archive-date=22 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251222102827/https://www.college-of-arms.gov.uk/RoyalWarrant2004.pdf |url-status=live }}</ref> His profile was taken down from the royal website the following day.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Longmire |first=Becca |date=31 October 2025 |title=Prince Andrew's name erased from official royal list after he's stripped of all titles |url=https://people.com/prince-andrew-erased-roll-of-the-peerage-after-stripped-royal-titles-11840928 |access-date=31 October 2025 |magazine=People |archive-date=31 October 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251031141016/https://people.com/prince-andrew-erased-roll-of-the-peerage-after-stripped-royal-titles-11840928 |url-status=live }}</ref>

On 3 November, [[Letters patent (United Kingdom)|letters patent]] were issued that removed the style "Royal Highness" and the title "prince" from Andrew,<ref name="Gazette-Removals" /> and on 1 December, a notice, backdated to 30 October, was published in ''[[The London Gazette]]'', stating that his appointments to the Order of the Garter and Royal Victorian Order had been annulled.<ref name="LG" /> He is to use the surname "Mountbatten-Windsor", in accordance with the [[Mountbatten-Windsor#Origin |1960 Privy Council declaration]] on the family surname by Queen Elizabeth II.<ref name=":2">{{cite news |last1=Davies |first1=Caroline |title=Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor's name to be hyphenated, as decreed by late queen |url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2025/nov/12/andrew-mountbatten-windsor-hyphenated-decreed-queen-elizabeth |access-date=12 November 2025 |work=The Guardian |date=12 November 2025}}</ref> On 13 December, the [[Ministry of Defence (United Kingdom)|Ministry of Defence]] announced that the [[Defence Council of the United Kingdom]] had removed Andrew's [[Vice-admiral (Royal Navy)|honorary rank of vice-admiral]], whereupon he reverted to the rank of [[Commander (Royal Navy)|commander]] (retired).<ref name=":1" /> Also in December, [[New Zealand]] stripped him of the [[New Zealand 1990 Commemoration Medal|1990 Commemoration Medal]].<ref name=NZP>{{Cite web |last=Vance |first=Andrea |date=25 February 2026 |title=Andrew stripped of Waitangi commemoration medal |url=https://www.thepost.co.nz/politics/360956353/andrew-stripped-waitangi-commemoration-medal |access-date=2026-02-26 |website=www.thepost.co.nz}}</ref>

==== 2026 ====
On 19 February 2026, after Andrew was arrested on suspicion of misconduct in public office, the [[Norwegian royal family]] announced that he had relinquished and returned his appointment as a member of the [[Order of St. Olav]].<ref name=":3" />

Andrew remains eighth in the [[line of succession to the British throne]] as of 2026.<ref>{{cite news |date=31 October 2025 |title=Andrew's move out of Royal Lodge may not happen until new year |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c2emmdnw82yo |access-date=1 November 2025 |work=BBC News |archive-date=30 December 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251230050411/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c2emmdnw82yo |url-status=live }}</ref> The UK government has indicated that it is considering legislation to remove him from the line of succession, a step that would require the consent of all Commonwealth realms.<ref name="bbc-andrew-succession-2026">{{Cite news |date=20 February 2026 |title=Government considers removing Andrew from royal line of succession |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1kgv837wvdo |access-date=24 February 2026 |work=BBC News |language=en-GB |last=Wheeler |first=Richard |last2=Eardley |first2=Nick}}</ref> The governments of Canada, Australia, and New Zealand have stated that they would support such a measure.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/czd87d0jq37o|title=Canada's PM calls for Andrew to be removed from line of succession|work=BBC News|first=Fiona|last=Nimoni|date=7 March 2026|access-date=7 March 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Davies |first=Maia |date=23 February 2026 |title=Australia backs removing Andrew from royal line of succession |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr5lrny83dgo |access-date=23 February 2026 |work=BBC News}}</ref><ref name="BBC-NZ-Andrew-2026">{{cite news |last=Atkinson |first=Simon |last2=Wilson |first2=Tabby |date=23 February 2026 |title=New Zealand backs removing Andrew from royal line of succession |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c4gjz2p8wr2o |access-date=24 February 2026 |work=BBC News}}</ref>

As the third [[British subject]] of full age in the line of succession [[Domicile (law)|domiciled]] in the UK, Andrew holds the position of [[Counsellor of State]], although it is unlikely he would be called upon to exercise any royal functions in that capacity because he is no longer a working member of the royal family;<ref name="Guardian-payments" /> he would automatically cease to hold the role if removed from the line of succession.<ref name="bbc-andrew-succession-2026" />

